meta twice at a population the cap raise took\n *  1000 → 20 000 — the same double scan the Python leg removed\n *  (`capture_spool._saturated_for_metas`). One scan, both answers; the\n *  arithmetic lives HERE only, so the two legs and the two callers cannot drift.\n */\nfunction saturatedForMetas(\n  dir: string,\n  metas: SpoolMeta[],\n  bounds: SpoolBounds = DEFAULT_BOUNDS,\n  projection: {\n    projectedBytes?: number;\n    replacesSessionId?: string;\n    addsEntry?: boolean;\n    pendingOrphanBytes?: number;\n  } = {},\n  unreadableBytes = 0,\n  unreadableCount = 0,\n): boolean {\n  // Bytes `pruneSpool` may reclaim WITHOUT spending an undelivered entry: the\n  // orphan class is byte-evictable only (see below).\n  const reclaimableOrphanBytes = orphanPaths(dir).reduce((sum, path) => sum + fileSize(path), 0);\n  let total =\n    metas.reduce((sum, m) => sum + spoolEntryBytes(dir, m.session_id), 0) +\n    // #12483: a RETAINED-UNREADABLE entry occupies bytes and can never be\n    // evicted, so it is added to the total and NOT to the reclaimable credit.\n    // Without this the byte ceiling stopped enforcing the moment an entry became\n    // unreadable — measured 5,228 bytes in `entries/` against a 1,000-byte\n    // ceiling with the predicate answering False.\n    unreadableBytes +\n    // Bytes a resync is about to quarantine are NOT in the projection (#14347\n    // review, cycle 2, finding 3): they become an `.orphan` AFTER the decision,\n    // so an admitted resync could land the spool over the byte ceiling and the\n    // `pruneSpool` on the SAME call would then evict the orphan it had just\n    // promised to keep. They are NOT reclaimable credit either — credit would say\n    // \"prune may delete them\", which is the outcome being avoided.\n    (projection.pendingOrphanBytes ?? 0) +\n    reclaimableOrphanBytes;\n  const { projectedBytes, replacesSessionId } = projection;\n  // The COUNT answer and the BYTE answer are different questions (#14347\n  // review, cycle 2, finding 4). The caller's answer is the POST-WRITE entry\n  // count: `addsEntry` is `true` only when the session is not on disk at all —\n  // the write path passes `!entryOnDisk` (#14347 review, cycle 5, finding 1), so\n  // an unreadable, corrupt or meta-less-log REPLACEMENT adds nothing. It is NOT\n  // `prior === undefined`: that operand was written when `parsedCount` was\n  // `metas.length` alone, and it now double-counts the unreadable class, which\n  // `unreadableCount` below already carries. Defaults keep every existing\n  // caller's behaviour. (The wording is the Python twin's, `capture_spool.py`\n  // `_admission_saturated` — see #14347 review, cycle 7, finding 1: this block\n  // stated the operand cycle 5 had already removed, twelve lines above the rule\n  // it contradicted, so a reader believed whichever leg they opened first.)\n  const addsEntry =\n    projection.addsEntry ?? (projection.replacesSessionId === undefined);\n  // #12483 said \"both ceilings\": `metas.length` cannot see a read-refused or\n  // corrupt entry either, so counting only parsed metas admitted writes past\n  // `maxEntries` without limit (measured: 6 unreadable metas against a ceiling of\n  // 3). Those entries are permanent — no meta to rank, never evicted — so they\n  // belong in the TOTAL and in the FLOOR, exactly like the byte term.\n  const parsedCount = metas.length + unreadableCount;\n  let strict = false;\n  let count = parsedCount;\n  if (projectedBytes === undefined && replacesSessionId === undefined) {\n    // STRICT: the question is whether ADDING an entry would cross the ceiling, so\n    // a spool exactly AT the count ceiling is already saturated.\n    strict = true;\n    if (parsedCount < bounds.maxEntries && total - reclaimableOrphanBytes < bounds.maxTotalBytes)\n      return false;\n  } else {\n    count = parsedCount + (addsEntry ? 1 : 0);\n    if (replacesSessionId !== undefined) {\n      // A GROWTH replaces this entry's bytes instead of adding a second copy of\n      // them, and adds no count.\n      total -= spoolEntryBytes(dir, replacesSessionId);\n    }\n    total += projectedBytes ?? 0;\n    if (count <= bounds.maxEntries && total - reclaimableOrphanBytes <= bounds.maxTotalBytes)\n      return false;\n  }\n  // Over a ceiling that the ORPHAN CREDIT could not absorb. Only a FILED entry\n  // may make room now: its content is already on the wire, so dropping it costs\n  // nothing a reader cannot re-fetch, while an UNDELIVERED entry is the only copy\n  // of a conversation that never reached the graph and `discardEntry` UNLINKS it.\n  // The entry being written can never reclaim ITSELF: `pruneSpool` is told to keep\n  // it (`keepSessionId`), so counting it would admit a write nothing can make room\n  // for.\n  //\n  // ⛔ BUT \"A FILED ENTRY EXISTS\" IS NOT \"ROOM CAN BE MADE\" (#12483). Two classes\n  // are permanent by design: an UNDELIVERED entry (never evicted) and a\n  // retained-UNREADABLE one (no meta to rank, and the state is reversible). Once\n  // those two alone carry the spool over a ceiling, evicting the filed class\n  // cannot bring it back under — so admitting the write spends FILED entries for\n  // room that does not exist, one write at a time, while the spool keeps\n  // reporting \"not saturated\". The FLOOR below is the size AFTER evicting\n  // everything the spool is allowed to evict; if the floor is still over, the\n  // answer is REFUSED (#10175's rule: refuse new work, never destroy the queue to\n  // admit it). Mirrors `capture_spool._saturated_for_metas`.\n  const filed = metas.filter(\n    (m) => entryFiled(m) && m.session_id !== replacesSessionId,\n  );\n  const floorBytes =\n    total -\n    filed.reduce((sum, m) => sum + spoolEntryBytes(dir, m.session_id), 0) -\n    reclaimableOrphanBytes;\n  // ⛔ THE STRICT ARM'S FLOOR MUST CARRY THE `+1` THE WRITER WILL ADD (#14347\n  // review, cycle 6, finding 1). `strict` means \"no projection was offered\", and\n  // every caller of that arm is asking the question about a NEW capture —\n  // `captureHealthLine` above all — so the post-write count is\n  // `parsedCount + 1`, exactly as `admissionSaturated` computes it for a fresh\n  // session. Without the `+1` the floor is one short of the writer's, and the two\n  // disagree whenever `parsedCount - filed.length === maxEntries` with at least\n  // one filed entry: measured, three readable undelivered + one FILED at\n  // `maxEntries: 3` gave health `DRAINING (3 queued)` while the writer refused\n  // every NEW capture with `spool_saturated`. The writer is right (after prune\n  // evicts the filed entry the count is 4, still over a ceiling of 3, so room\n  // cannot be made and #10175 requires the refusal) — the health line was the\n  // false PASS. The byte axis is deliberately NOT given the same `+1`: a new\n  // capture's bytes are unknown to a caller that offered no projection, and the\n  // `>=` comparison is the conservative stand-in for them.\n  const floorCount = count + (strict ? 1 : 0) - filed.length;\n  const overCount = strict ? parsedCount >= bounds.maxEntries : count > bounds.maxEntries;\n  const overBytes =\n    strict\n      ? total - reclaimableOrphanBytes >= bounds.maxTotalBytes\n      : total - reclaimableOrphanBytes > bounds.maxTotalBytes;\n  if (overBytes && (strict ? floorBytes >= bounds.maxTotalBytes : floorBytes > bounds.maxTotalBytes))\n    return true;\n  if (overCount && floorCount > bounds.maxEntries) return true;\n  return filed.length === 0;\n}\n\n/**\n * The per-attempt OUTCOME CONTRACT (#5051) — the TS mirror of\n * `tortoise/capture_receipts.py::capture_outcome`, asserted equal to it over\n * the full status space by `tests/test_capture_spool.py`'s parity test.\n *\n * The Python spool and this hook share ONE spool directory\n * (`~/.tortoise/capture-spool`), so a status the two answer differently about\n * is a capture one leg keeps and the other terminalises.\n *\n * `landed`     — 2xx: the commit is durable. Mint the receipt, retire the entry.\n * `retryable`  — the MOMENT was wrong, not the bytes: no status, non-finite,\n *                3xx, 402, 408, 409, 425, 429, EVERY 5xx (503 and 504 are the\n *                same class — #4925).\n * `policy`     — 401/403/407: a REVERSIBLE authorization/entitlement state,\n *                resolved by fixing the credential or the org state, never by\n *                re-sending the same bytes (#4895). KEEP it; never\n *                terminalise it.\n * `permanent`  — these bytes can never succeed: 400, 404, 405, 406, 413, 415,\n *                422, any other 4xx. Terminalise — record + KEEP (#7708).\n *\n * `detail` is accepted for signature symmetry with the Python leg and is\n * deliberately NOT interpreted (see `classifyFailure` below: keying on server\n * prose would couple this client to a wording it ships independently of).\n */\nexport function captureOutcome(\n  status: number | null | undefined,\n  detail?: string,\n): \"landed\" | \"retryable\" | \"policy\" | \"permanent\" {\n  void detail;\n  // TOTAL over \"no status\": `undefined`, `null`, `NaN`/`Infinity` and — via\n  // `typeof` — any non-number a corrupt caller passes. `null >= 300` is FALSE,\n  // so an unguarded fall-through sent a transport failure to \"permanent\" and\n  // unlinked the capture while the Python leg kept it.\n  if (typeof status !== \"number\" || !Number.isFinite(status)) return \"retryable\";\n  if (status >= 200 && status < 300) return \"landed\";\n  if (status >= 300 && status < 400) return \"retryable\";\n  if (status >= 500) return \"retryable\";\n  // 401/403/407 are a refusal of the CALLER, not of the bytes.\n  if (status === 401 || status === 403 || status === 407) return \"policy\";\n  if (status === 402 || status === 408 || status === 425 || status === 429) return \"retryable\";\n  if (status === 409) return \"retryable\";\n  return \"permanent\";\n}\n\n/**\n * May an entry be retried again? — the SPOOL's question (`#5051`).\n *\n * True for every class except `permanent`, so a `policy` refusal (a suspended\n * org's 403, a rotated key's 401) is KEPT and retried once the caller fixes the\n * credential or the org state, instead of being stranded terminal.\n */\nexport function mayKeepEntry(status: number | null | undefined, detail?: string): boolean {\n  return captureOutcome(status, detail) !== \"permanent\";\n}\n\n/**\n * Classify an upload failure — a PROJECTION of {@link captureOutcome}.\n *\n * The status→class table lives ONCE, in `captureOutcome`. This keeps the\n * narrower, pre-existing answer its callers ask — *\"may this become valid by\n * waiting / is this a retry?\"* — so the Python/Pi parity contract on\n * `classifyFailure` (pinned by `tests/test_capture_spool.py`) is unchanged by\n * the re-declaration. Consequence sites must NOT read this: whether an entry\n * may be kept is {@link mayKeepEntry}.\n *\n * TRANSIENT (retry with backoff): no status (network / timeout), 5xx, 3xx (a\n * redirect on a stored api_url must not delete the capture), the retryable 4xx\n * family (402 quota-refusal, 408 request-timeout, 425 too-early, 429\n * rate-limit), and EVERY 409.\n *\n * \"No status\" is TOTAL, and it has to be: `undefined` (the fetch never\n * resolved), `null` (a transport/fetchImpl that models absence as null), and a\n * non-finite value (an unparseable status). A missing status is a network\n * condition, never a server verdict.\n *\n * POLICY (\"policy\"): 401/403/407 — a reversible authorization/entitlement\n * refusal (#4895).\n *\n * On this idempotent upsert a 409 is either #3713's in-flight concurrency\n * condition (retry then replays) or a policy state (recording disabled) that\n * the user can reverse — a capture must not be destroyed because recording was\n * briefly off. Prose-matching the server's in-flight message is deliberately\n * avoided: the client ships independently of the server, so a reworded detail\n * would silently turn #3713's benign 409 into a lost write.\n *\n * 402 is TRANSIENT (#4714), and the Python leg already classifies it so. The\n * hosted quota gate refuses a capture whose *estimated* point cost would cross\n * the org's cap, and `est` is computed from the INCOMING capture — so the\n * identical capture succeeds the moment a node is freed or the tier changes,\n * exactly the \"becomes valid by waiting\" property that defines transient here.\n * Classified permanent, `flushSpool` routed it to `discardEntry`, which\n * UNLINKS the meta and the turn log. Both legs share ONE spool directory\n * (`~/.tortoise/capture-spool`), so leaving 402 permanent here re-opens the\n * data loss the Python fix closes: a capture the Python drain correctly defers\n * is destroyed by the next Pi drain. Retry is bounded by the spool's count/byte\n * bound and the ENTRY's `backoffDelay` — carried across turns, so an\n * actively-growing session is retried on the backoff clock rather than once per\n * turn. Under sustained over-quota nothing can file, so nothing is evictable:\n * the ONLY evictable class is an already-filed entry (oldest-first within it),\n * and an all-undelivered spool SATURATES — new work is refused and recorded\n * (`spool_saturated`) while the backlog is kept (capture-loss, 2026-11-01). Both\n * legs state that one policy (`capture_spool.py`).\n *\n * ⚠️ #4614 gave the refusal a machine-readable CATEGORY\n * (`detail.code === \"quota_exceeded\"`) so a caller no longer has to match the\n * message text. This classifier still keys on the STATUS, deliberately: the\n * category is for REPORTING and for surfaces that can act on it, and treating\n * a `quota_exceeded` 402 as terminal here would re-open #4714's data loss. The\n * two legs must keep answering this the same way (`capture_spool.py`).\n *\n * PERMANENT (record + terminal + KEEP — #7708): every other 4xx — a malformed\n * payload or an\n * out-of-range turn count never becomes valid by waiting. The verdict is\n * unchanged; `flushSpool` no longer UNLINKS a permanently-refused entry.\n */\nexport function classifyFailure(\n  status: number | null | undefined,\n  detail?: string,\n): \"retry\" | \"policy\" | \"permanent\" {\n  const verdict = captureOutcome(status, detail);\n  // `landed` is unreachable from a correct caller (every leg checks `ok`\n  // first) and projects onto this function's PRE-EXISTING answer for a 2xx\n  // (`\"permanent\"` — a 2xx is not a failure any caller should route), so this\n  // stays a pure re-declaration rather than a behaviour change. Every other\n  // class passes through unchanged.\n  if (verdict === \"retryable\") return \"retry\";\n  return verdict === \"landed\" ? \"permanent\" : verdict;\n}\n\n/** `attempts` as a small non-negative int, however corrupt the stored value is.\n *  Clamped because `backoffDelay` computes `2 ** (n - 1)` from it; the backoff\n *  saturates (30 s * 2**10 > 6 h) long before this bound. */\nexport function clampAttempts(value: unknown): number {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0\n    ? Math.min(Math.floor(value), MAX_ATTEMPTS)\n    : 0;\n}\n\n/** `next_attempt_at_ms` as a FINITE epoch-ms value — 0 means \"retry now\".\n *  A non-finite window is not a window: `Infinity > nowMs` is true forever, and\n *  the carry-forward would preserve it across every turn, making a growing\n *  session permanently un-fileable while every surface says \"will retry\". */\nexport function clampWindow(value: unknown): number {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0 ? value : 0;\n}\n\n/** The prior entry's window, bounded to what the write path can produce.\n *  `clampWindow` makes it finite; this makes it PLAUSIBLE. A legitimate window\n *  is at most `written_at + RETRY_MAX_MS` (`backoffDelay` saturates there), so\n *  anything further out is corrupt — and because it is CARRIED forward it would\n *  be re-written on every turn and strand the entry permanently, silently. */\nexport function carriedWindow(value: unknown): number {\n  const window = clampWindow(value);\n  return window <= Date.now() + RETRY_MAX_MS ? window : 0;\n}\n\n/** Exponential backoff for attempt N (1-based), capped at RETRY_MAX_MS. */\nexport function backoffDelay(attempts: number): number {\n  const exp = Math.max(0, clampAttempts(attempts) - 1);\n  return Math.min(RETRY_BASE_MS * 2 ** exp, RETRY_MAX_MS);\n}\n\nfunction writeMeta(dir: string, meta: SpoolMeta): void {\n  try {\n    atomicWrite(metaPathFor(dir, meta.session_id), `${JSON.stringify(meta, null, 2)}\\n`);\n  } catch {\n    // best effort — the entry simply replays again next opportunity\n  }\n}\n\n/** The entry's `terminal_failure` marker, or `undefined` — the ONE total read.\n *\n *  A truthy NON-object value (a hand-edited / foreign / corrupt `meta.json`:\n *  `1`, `true`, `\"yes\"`, `[]`) is NOT a marker. Reading the field by truthiness\n *  alone made such an entry append to `summary.terminal` on EVERY flush — never\n *  retried, silent on every surface (#8531 review). The safe reading of an\n *  unusable marker is \"not terminal, retry\" (the `clampWindow` reading): the\n *  entry stays filable, and a genuine refusal re-marks it on the next attempt.\n *  `[]` is excluded explicitly — `typeof [] === \"object\"` and an empty array is\n *  truthy, so a bare object check would still strand it. */\nfunction terminalMarker(meta: SpoolMeta): { reason?: string; detail?: string } | undefined {\n  const marker: unknown = meta.terminal_failure;\n  if (!marker || typeof marker !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(marker)) return undefined;\n  return marker as { reason?: string; detail?: string };\n}\n\n/**\n * #12379 / #12460: the spool meta key the drain uses for \"the server never\n * recorded how extraction ended\". MIRRORED byte-for-byte from the Python leg\n * (`capture_spool.UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY` / `UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_VALUE`) — the\n * two legs write ONE shared meta, so a different spelling here would mean\n * neither leg could see the other's marker.\n */\nconst UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY = \"extraction_outcome\";\nconst UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_VALUE = \"unknown\";\n\n/**\n * The `confirmed` verdicts that mean the capture's ending is settled, so a marker\n * standing over one of them is stale.\n *\n * MIRRORED from `capture_spool.RECORDED_OUTCOME_VERDICTS` — `filed` is\n * `extracted >= 1` (#12374's FILED verdict); `unextracted` is the confirmation\n * read's \"durable, no points visible yet\" (`session_confirm.UNEXTRACTED`) — a\n * verdict that fires on any zero count BEHIND A TURN MATCH (a non-empty mismatch or\n * a grown set returns `TURNS_MISSING`, an absent one `UNKNOWN` — both defer), not\n * only on a store-only capture.\n * ⚠️ `unextracted` rests on the confirmation READ matching the posted\n * turns, NOT on the server having recorded an outcome — that read DOES consult\n * `extraction.outcome_known` since #12374 (`session_confirm._extraction_verdict`\n * returns `PENDING` on an explicit `false`), so it is as strong as the read; the\n * surviving residual is a served detail with NO `extraction` block at all, and\n * #12374's PENDING branch is what qualifies the read. Everything else — `pending` (still unknown), `replayed` (already captured,\n * says nothing about extraction), an absent verdict, a malformed body — leaves\n * the marker alone.\n */\nconst RECORDED_OUTCOME_VERDICTS = [\"filed\", \"unextracted\"] as const;\n\n/**\n * #12379: does this 2xx body say the EXTRACTION OUTCOME was never recorded?\n *\n * THE one definition of the state #12379 owns on this leg; the Python leg's\n * `outcome_unrecorded` is the same predicate over the same wire body. It is\n * NOT \"extracted nothing\": a keyless capture (#4188) or a team with extraction\n * switched off (#4258) genuinely produced no points and the server SAYS so\n * (`outcome_known: true`) — pricing those as unrecorded would offer a re-attempt\n * for a session that may already have extracted (the #12362 bug).\n *\n * ⚠️ `outcome_known` is read off the STORED session (`capture_ok is not None`),\n * not per write: a keyless RE-capture writes no new outcome, so a session whose\n * `capture_ok` is `null` (built by a driver that never records one) stays\n * `outcome_known: false` — mirroring the Python leg — while one that already\n * carries a recorded lane keeps reading true.\n *\n * Deliberately fail-safe: only the explicit marker counts, so a body from a\n * tree without #12362, a malformed one, or `undefined` never FABRICATES a trace.\n */\nfunction outcomeUnrecorded(body: unknown): boolean {\n  return Boolean(\n    body && typeof body === \"object\" && !Array.isArray(body) &&\n      (body as Record<string, unknown>)[UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY] === UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_VALUE,\n  );\n}\n\n/**\n * #12460: does this 2xx body settle how the capture ended?\n *\n * The inverse question, and the ONLY evidence allowed to CLEAR the marker.\n * A CLOSED allow-list, never \"anything that is not pending\": a typo, a `null`, a\n * future verdict or an uninformative body must leave the marker alone, because\n * clearing over one of them deletes the only trace on the routine\n * resume/clear/compact path where the server answers `replayed` (measured\n * in #12416's review round 1: marker set → replay filing → marker gone, while\n * the condition provably persisted).\n *\n * MIRRORED from `capture_spool.outcome_recorded` — one predicate per leg, and\n * both must stay identical or the two writes to one shared meta flip the marker\n * against each other.\n *\n * ⛔ Two body SHAPES carry this verdict, and only one of them was believed by\n * the first version of this port — it read only the one that never arrives here:\n *\n *  1. The **Python drain's** vocabulary (`confirmed: \"filed\" | \"unextracted\"`).\n *     That body is synthesised by `__main__._session_post` for a refusal that\n *     turned out to have committed — the Pi leg POSTs to `/v1/sessions` directly\n *     and never sees it. Kept because it is the same question and a body carrying\n *     it must be read alike; on this leg it is inert today.\n *  2. The **capture receipt** this leg actually receives — the `resp = {…}`\n *     dict in `hosted_api._capture_session_impl` (`tortoise/hosted_api.py`,\n *     `resp = {` .. `\"first_capture\": …}`): `{session_id, turns, extracted,\n *     points, extraction_mode, …}` — NO `confirmed`, NO `extraction_outcome`.\n *     So the clear has to be derived from the receipt's own evidence, or this\n *     port is dead code that closes the issue without changing behaviour\n *     (measured in this lane's verification: a mirrored-only predicate never\n *     fires in production).\n *\n * The receipt clause, the SAME evidence the Python leg's verdicts rest on\n * (`session_confirm.confirm_capture`):\n *\n *  - `extracted >= 1` ⇒ extraction RAN ⇒ `FILED` ⇒ recorded. This is the\n *    dominant resolved case, and the one #12460's symptom is about. A REPLAY\n *    reports `extracted: 0`, so the routine resume/clear/compact path can never\n *    satisfy it.\n *\n * ⛔ `extracted: 0` in a normal mode is NOT on the list. The capture handler that\n * the transport bound abandoned may still be extracting — the server's own\n * \"there is no outcome yet\" state — so a zero is AMBIGUOUS, not a negative, and\n * treating it as evidence is the #12362 bug wearing the other mask. The marker\n * stands, which over-reports an unknown state and never hides a resolved one.\n *\n * ⛔ NEITHER IS A STORE-ONLY `extraction_mode`. `no-provider` /\n * `extraction-disabled` says only that THIS REQUEST skipped extraction: the\n * server gates its durable outcome record on `not session_existed`\n * (`hosted_api.py` — `_record_session_state = (not session_existed) if store_only`),\n * so a keyless RE-capture of an existing session records NOTHING while still\n * reporting `extraction_mode: \"no-provider\"` with `extracted: 0`. Clearing on\n * that destroys the only trace of a session whose outcome was never recorded —\n * exactly the false-PASS direction this trace exists to prevent (found in review\n * of PR #12507, verified against `hosted_api.py`).\n *\n * ⚠️ RESIDUAL, and it is NOT closed by the Python leg either: `confirm_capture`\n * matches the posted turns and reads `extracted`, and it DOES consult\n * `extraction.outcome_known` through `_extraction_verdict` (since #12374) — so its\n * `unextracted` verdict is qualified for a served detail that carries the block.\n * What no leg's `unextracted` verdict can distinguish is a served detail with NO\n * `extraction` block at all (a pre-#3520 server, a fake): `outcome_known` reads\n * `None`, and a `confirmed: \"unextracted\"` body can therefore still clear this\n * marker over an unrecorded outcome. That is pre-existing (#12379's accepted\n * decision) and is stated here so this paragraph's own reasoning is not read as\n * \"the other leg is authoritative\".\n */\nfunction outcomeRecorded(body: unknown): boolean {\n  if (!body || typeof body !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(body)) return false;\n  const receipt = body as Record<string, unknown>;\n  const verdict = receipt.confirmed;\n  if (\n    typeof verdict === \"string\" &&\n    (RECORDED_OUTCOME_VERDICTS as readonly string[])\n      .includes(verdict.trim().toLowerCase())\n  ) {\n    return true;\n  }\n  return extractionObserved(receipt.extracted);\n}\n\n/**\n * Did the server report that extraction RAN — `session_confirm.session_extracted`\n * plus its `>= 1` test?\n *\n * ONE definition, read by BOTH directions: the CLEAR (`outcomeRecorded`) and the\n * confirm-on-refusal read's `_extraction_verdict` precedence\n * (`refusalOutcomeVerdict`). Two spellings of the same clause is how the\n * two directions come apart: the round-2 review of #12603 caught the precedence\n * arm re-spelling it with a comment claiming a parity it did not have.\n *\n * A bool, a string or a non-numeric reads as 0, so an odd count can never\n * satisfy it. ⚠️ NOT identical to the Python predicate on one input: JSON has no\n * float/integer distinction here (`JSON.parse('{\"extracted\": 4.0}')` gives `4`,\n * and `Number.isInteger(4)` is true) while Python's `isinstance(4.0, int)` is\n * false — so a float-spelled count counts as observed on this leg and not on the\n * other. Unreachable in practice, because the only producer is\n * `hosted_api`'s `\"extracted\": len(extracted)`, an integer token.\n */\nfunction extractionObserved(value: unknown): boolean {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isInteger(value) && value >= 1;\n}\n\n/**\n * #2100: is this entry already FILED — has the server accepted EXACTLY this\n * content, for this lane and this client stamp?\n *\n * ONE definition, read by three places: the drain's skip clause, the\n * post-POST CAS re-read, and the per-box health count. A health line that\n * used a different notion of \"filed\" from the drain would describe a spool\n * nobody is draining — and a filed entry is NOT pending work: the success\n * path stamps `filed_*` and keeps the entry (nothing unlinks a filed\n * capture), so counting meta files measures spool OCCUPANCY, not backlog.\n */\nfunction entryFiled(meta: SpoolMeta): boolean {\n  return Boolean(\n    meta.filed_key && meta.filed_key === meta.capture_key &&\n      // Compared against the SAME normalisation the payload uses: the wire\n      // carries `finiteInstant(...)`, so comparing the RAW disk value can never\n      // match for a value the guards refuse — and the drain never rewrites the\n      // meta, so such an entry would re-POST its full transcript on EVERY\n      // drain, forever (#4714 amplification).\n      normaliseLane(meta.filed_lane) === normaliseLane(meta.capture_lane) &&\n      (meta.filed_stamp ?? undefined) === finiteInstant(meta.client_captured_at),\n  );\n}\n\n/**\n * Bounded allowance for the confirm-on-refusal READ (#12603), in ms.\n *\n * ⛔ NOT `REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` (10 s). This read runs inside `session_shutdown`\n * (and every background flush) on the REFUSAL path, i.e. exactly where the\n * transport bound has already been spent once; a second 10 s wait would double\n * the stall. `PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS`'s 2 s is the precedent — the read is a single\n * indexed GET that the #12374 measurement answered in ~0.5 s.\n *\n * Worst case added to ONE pass: `FLUSH_MAX_ATTEMPTS` (8) refusals × 2 s = 16 s\n * on top of the pass's POST budget. Every read is per-ENTRY (once, after that\n * entry's refusal), never per-turn, and the pass budget is still checked\n * BETWEEN entries.\n */\nexport const CONFIRM_READ_TIMEOUT_MS = 2_000;\n\n/**\n * The refusal statuses whose session can NEVER be confirmed — MIRRORED from\n * `capture_receipts.REFUSAL_STATUSES` (402/403/503), the family the Python leg's\n * `_session_post._refused` excludes BY NAME before it confirms.\n *\n * #4675's recorded 2xx-only rule: a `retryable` refusal is not proof that\n * nothing committed, EXCEPT for these three, where the server has said the\n * session itself is the problem (quota / credential / service). Reading there\n * would be a round trip that can only answer 404, and on a quota-blocked box it\n * would be one per pass — while the two legs would then disagree about whether a\n * 402 confirms. Uniformity is deliberate: a reviewer must not have to work out\n * why one leg confirms a 402 and the other does not.\n * `tests/test_pi_capture_hooks.py` pins this list against the Python table.\n */\nconst NO_CONFIRM_READ_STATUSES: readonly number[] = [402, 403, 503];\n\n/** The served-body redaction marker (``tortoise.security._REDACTION_VALUE``).\n * A body is never delimiter-neutralized, so this square form is the only marker\n * a BODY can carry. */\nconst SERVED_BODY_REDACTION_MARKER = /\\[REDACTED:[^\\[\\]]*\\]/;\n/** The served-ROLE redaction marker: `_capture_turn_frame` neutralizes the\n * framing delimiters in the role (#5445), so a redacted role is served as\n * `(REDACTED:<kind>)`. Recognising the square form in a role would treat a role\n * the user LITERALLY typed as `(REDACTED:foo)` as a wildcard. */\nconst SERVED_ROLE_REDACTION_MARKER = /\\(REDACTED:[^()]*\\)/;\n\n/**\n * `GET /v1/sessions/<id>` — the ONE read `session verify` already makes\n * (`session_verify._session_detail`), and the only new call this leg makes.\n *\n * TOTAL: every failure mode (`no key`, non-2xx, no body, unparseable body,\n * transport error, abort) returns `undefined` — never a throw and never a\n * fabricated body. The caller's safe direction is \"inconclusive = stamp\n * nothing\", so a read that cannot answer must be indistinguishable from one\n * that was never asked.\n *\n * ⚠️ ONE attempt. `session_confirm.confirm_capture` (the Python leg's read)\n * makes `DEFAULT_ATTEMPTS` = 2 attempts `DEFAULT_DELAY_S` = 1.0 s apart with a\n * `DEFAULT_READ_TIMEOUT_S` = 5 s bound each; this is a single 2 s read, because\n * it runs inside `session_shutdown`'s budget. The asymmetry can only LOSE a\n * trace, never fabricate one: a read that 503s (`graph_unavailable`) or 404s once\n * and would answer on Python's second attempt stamps nothing here, and the next\n * pass re-POSTs the entry, gets a REPLAY receipt, and (correctly) clears nothing.\n * Stated here rather than closed because retrying would add up to 4 s per refused\n * entry to a hook path.\n *\n * ⚠️ The id is PERCENT-ENCODED rather than spliced raw into the path (the\n * Python leg's `f\"/v1/sessions/{session_id}\"`). Pi session ids are usually\n * opaque, but `localSessionId` (#9599) anchors an id to the transcript PATH, so\n * a slash-bearing id is reachable: raw splicing would read a DIFFERENT path (a\n * truncated id) and could return some other session's detail as if it were ours,\n * while encoding it makes the route a miss — which is the fail-safe direction\n * here. Neither leg can address such a session by id; this one just cannot be\n * confused about it.\n */\nasync function getSessionDetail(\n  cfg: CaptureConfig,\n  sessionId: string,\n  fetchImpl: FetchLike,\n  timeoutMs: number = CONFIRM_READ_TIMEOUT_MS,\n): Promise<unknown> {\n  if (!cfg.apiKey || !sessionId) return undefined;\n  const controller = new AbortController();\n  const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs);\n  try {\n    const res = await fetchImpl(\n      `${cfg.apiUrl}/v1/sessions/${encodeURIComponent(sessionId)}`,\n      {\n        method: \"GET\",\n        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${cfg.apiKey}` },\n        signal: controller.signal,\n      },\n    );\n    if (!res.ok) return undefined;\n    try {\n      return await res.json();\n    } catch {\n      // a 2xx with no readable JSON body carries no verdict\n      return undefined;\n    }\n  } catch {\n    return undefined;\n  } finally {\n    clearTimeout(timer);\n  }\n}\n\n/** The deterministic per-turn id — MIRRORED from\n * `session_confirm.turn_point_id` / `sdk._capture_turn_id` (`{session_id}_t{i}`),\n * the server's idempotency contract for a capture's turns. Pinned cross-language\n * by `tests/test_pi_capture_hooks.py::test_the_pi_turn_id_matches_session_confirm`\n * — the extension cannot import Python, so the literal needs a pin or the match\n * goes silently inert when the server's derivation moves. */\nfunction turnPointId(sessionId: string, index: number): string {\n  return `${sessionId}_t${index}`;\n}\n\n/**\n * The served `turn_points` as `{id: [role, content]}`, or `undefined` when the\n * block is absent/unusable.\n *\n * MIRRORS `session_confirm.turn_points`: a row that is not a mapping with a\n * non-empty string `id` is SKIPPED, a non-string `content` becomes a sentinel\n * that can never equal a real body, and a missing/odd `role` becomes `\"\"` —\n * never the expected role. Total over a malformed payload, because this runs\n * inside a failure path whose contract is \"never replace the honest error with\n * a traceback\".\n */\nfunction servedTurnPoints(detail: unknown): Map<string, [string, string]> | undefined {\n  if (!detail || typeof detail !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(detail)) {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n  const rows = (detail as Record<string, unknown>).turn_points;\n  if (!Array.isArray(rows)) return undefined;\n  const out = new Map<string, [string, string]>();\n  for (const row of rows) {\n    if (!row || typeof row !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(row)) continue;\n    const r = row as Record<string, unknown>;\n    const id = r.id;\n    if (typeof id !== \"string\" || !id) continue;\n    const content = r.content;\n    out.set(id, [\n      typeof r.role === \"string\" ? r.role : \"\",\n      typeof content === \"string\"\n        ? content\n        : \"\\u0000<no content on the returned row>\",\n    ]);\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\n/**\n * True when `stored` is `raw` with zero or more spans replaced by a redaction\n * marker.\n *\n * MIRRORS `session_confirm._stored_is_raw_with_markers` (#5394). The server\n * scrubs the text it stores with ITS `_SECRET_SHAPES` table, deployed\n * independently of this extension, so requiring byte identity makes every\n * capture containing a secret read as \"not ours\" — and the deferred entry never\n * terminalises. The literal (non-marker) spans must still match byte-for-byte,\n * in order, and each marker stands for at least one character: a missing turn,\n * a truncated body, or any difference OUTSIDE a marker still fails, which is\n * what keeps this a MATCH and not a wildcard.\n */\nfunction storedIsRawWithMarkers(\n  raw: string,\n  stored: string,\n  marker: RegExp,\n): boolean {\n  if (raw === stored) return true;\n  const span = new RegExp(marker.source, \"g\");\n  if (!span.test(stored)) return false;\n  span.lastIndex = 0;\n  const segments: Array<{ isMarker: boolean; text: string }> = [];\n  let last = 0;\n  for (let m = span.exec(stored); m !== null; m = span.exec(stored)) {\n    segments.push({ isMarker: false, text: stored.slice(last, m.index) });\n    segments.push({ isMarker: true, text: \"\" });\n    last = m.index + m[0].length;\n    if (m[0].length === 0) {\n      // Defensive: neither marker can match empty, but a future pattern that\n      // could must not spin this loop.\n      span.lastIndex += 1;\n    }\n  }\n  segments.push({ isMarker: false, text: stored.slice(last) });\n  let pos = 0;\n  const end = raw.length;\n  const final = segments.length - 1;\n  for (let i = 0; i < segments.length; i += 1) {\n    const seg = segments[i];\n    if (seg.isMarker) {\n      // Each marker stands for at least one raw character.\n      if (pos >= end) return false;\n      pos += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (i === 0) {\n      if (!raw.startsWith(seg.text, pos)) return false;\n      pos += seg.text.length;\n    } else if (i === final) {\n      // fullmatch: the trailing literal must END the raw string.\n      return end - seg.text.length >= pos && raw.endsWith(seg.text);\n    } else {\n      // Leftmost leaves the most room for a sequence with no upper bound but\n      // the final literal, so it is optimal — the same reading the Python leg\n      // documents and checks against the direct transcription.\n      const idx = raw.indexOf(seg.text, pos);\n      if (idx < 0) return false;\n      pos = idx + seg.text.length;\n    }\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n\n/**\n * The exact set the READER's inverse can consume at the start of a body: the\n * reader is `sdk._capture_turn_role_text`, whose pattern is the role prefix\n * followed by a GREEDY `\\s*`, so the served body has its leading run of\n * Python-`\\s` characters STRIPPED.\n *\n * ⛔ It is NOT `BLANK_CHARS`. That constant is the union of Python's\n * `str.strip()` set and JS `trim()`'s (see its note), and the union's one extra\n * member is **U+FEFF**, which Python's `re \\s` does NOT match — so stripping it\n * would make our expectation SHORTER than the body the server serves, and the\n * row would never match. Over-stripping is UNDER-matching in an equality test:\n * the safe direction is Python's set exactly, which is why this is derived from\n * `BLANK_CHARS` by removing that one member rather than by re-spelling the list\n * (and why `tests/test_pi_capture_hooks.py` pins that U+FEFF is the only\n * member of Python's `_CAPTURE_BLANK_CHARS` that its `re \\s` refuses).\n */\nconst FRAME_INVERSE_BLANKS = BLANK_CHARS.replace(\"\\uFEFF\", \"\");\n\n/**\n * The READER's inverse of the stored frame, applied to our own text: the server\n * serves a body with its leading Python-`\\s` run stripped (`_capture_turn_role_text`),\n * and `session_confirm.expected_turns` builds its expectation through that same\n * inverse — so the Python leg compares a lstripped body. A port that compares\n * the raw text never matches a turn whose content begins with whitespace, and\n * the whole trace goes silently inert for those captures (round-2 review of\n * #12603 measured exactly that).\n */\nfunction servedFrameInverse(body: string): string {\n  let start = 0;\n  while (start < body.length && FRAME_INVERSE_BLANKS.includes(body[start])) {\n    start += 1;\n  }\n  return body.slice(start);\n}\n\n/**\n * Does the served detail hold EXACTLY the turns we just POSTed?\n *\n * The `expected_turns` model (`session_confirm.py`): the KEY SETS must be\n * identical — a missing, extra, grown or re-windowed turn is a different\n * transcript — and each row's role and body must be our own text, tolerating\n * server-side redaction only (`storedIsRawWithMarkers`).\n *\n * ⛔ THE POSTED TURNS ARE WINDOWED FIRST (`clipTurnContent` + a role slice,\n * mirroring `sdk._capture_turn_window`), because that is the text the server\n * stores and `expected_turns` applies the window before comparing. An entry\n * spooled by the PYTHON leg, or one written before #4897 taught this leg to clip,\n * can carry an over-cap turn; comparing the RAW text there never matches, so the\n * fix would be silently inert for exactly those captures. The client clipper is\n * idempotent (`clipTurnContent(clipTurnContent(x)) === clipTurnContent(x)`) and\n * byte-pinned to the Python one, so windowing an already-clipped turn is a no-op.\n *\n * ⛔ This is what makes the read EVIDENCE about OUR capture rather than about\n * the session id. The id is `{session_id}_t{i}` — deterministic — so only the\n * text distinguishes a concurrent capture that grew the transcript.\n */\nfunction servedTurnsAreOurCapture(\n  detail: unknown,\n  sessionId: string,\n  turns: Turn[],\n): boolean {\n  const have = servedTurnPoints(detail);\n  if (!have || have.size === 0) return false;\n  if (have.size !== turns.length) return false;\n  for (let i = 0; i < turns.length; i += 1) {\n    const row = have.get(turnPointId(sessionId, i));\n    if (!row) return false;\n    const [servedRole, servedBody] = row;\n    // The writer frames the stored text `[<role>] <body>` and neutralizes the\n    // framing delimiters IN THE ROLE only (#5445). The READER's inverse then\n    // strips the frame's leading whitespace run, so the body is compared through\n    // that inverse (`servedFrameInverse(clipTurnContent(...))`), not raw. The\n    // role is capped before the brackets are rewritten, the order the writer\n    // applies.\n    //\n    // ⛔ CAP IN CODE POINTS, not UTF-16 units: Python caps with\n    // `_normalize_turn_role(role)[:cap]`, which counts CODE POINTS. A `.slice()`\n    // counts code units, so a role carrying astral characters (an emoji-only\n    // role, say) would be capped at half the length Python allows — and this is\n    // an EQUALITY test, so the expectation comes out short and the row never\n    // matches. That direction is fail-safe (no stamp), which is precisely why it\n    // would never be noticed: no red, no alarm, the trace just goes inert for\n    // that capture. `clipTurnContent` is code-point safe on purpose for the same\n    // reason.\n    const wantRole = Array.from(\n      typeof turns[i].role === \"string\" ? turns[i].role : \"unknown\",\n    )\n      .slice(0, TURN_MAX_CHARS)\n      .join(\"\")\n      .replace(/\\[/g, \"(\")\n      .replace(/\\]/g, \")\");\n    if (!storedIsRawWithMarkers(wantRole, servedRole, SERVED_ROLE_REDACTION_MARKER)) {\n      return false;\n    }\n    if (!storedIsRawWithMarkers(servedFrameInverse(clipTurnContent(turns[i].content)),\n                                servedBody, SERVED_BODY_REDACTION_MARKER)) {\n      return false;\n    }\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n\n/** #12603: the confirm-on-refusal read's three answers. See\n *  `refusalOutcomeVerdict`. */\nexport type RefusalOutcomeVerdict = \"unrecorded\" | \"recorded\" | \"inconclusive\";\n\n/**\n * #12603: after a RETRYABLE refusal, what did the server RECORD about extraction\n * for the capture we just posted? Three-valued, and both directions read it:\n *\n * - `\"unrecorded\"` — an explicit `extraction.outcome_known === false` over a\n *   turn-matched read. STAMPS.\n * - `\"recorded\"` — a turn-matched read that proves an outcome exists: an\n *   integer `extracted >= 1` (`_extraction_verdict`'s `FILED`), or an explicit\n *   `outcome_known === true` (its `UNEXTRACTED`). CLEARS.\n * - `\"inconclusive\"` — anything else (no/unreadable body, turn mismatch,\n *   missing or non-boolean `outcome_known`). Decides NOTHING.\n *\n * ⛔ THE CLEAR DIRECTION IS NOT OPTIONAL, and round 4 of this change's review\n * measured why. `_extraction_verdict` answering `FILED` makes the Python leg's\n * `_refused` synthesise `{\"confirmed\": \"filed\"}`, which `capture_spool._flush_one`\n * CLEARS on. If this leg read the same detail and only declined to stamp, one\n * shared meta would hold a marker the other leg had already disproved — a\n * standing false alarm on exactly the #12374 shape (504 → stamp on pass 1 →\n * extraction finishes → pass 2's refusal read shows points), and nothing later\n * clears it, because a stamped entry's eventual filing is a REPLAY (`extracted:\n * 0`), which `outcomeRecorded` correctly refuses. Both legs now clear on the\n * same read, for the same reason.\n *\n * ⛔ THE MARKER'S ONLY PRODUCER ON THIS LEG. Before this, the key's sole producer\n * was Python-CLI's `__main__._session_post._refused`, which this leg never calls:\n * `flushSpool`'s `res.body` can only be a 2xx receipt (`{session_id, turns,\n * extracted, …}` — no `outcome_known`, no `extraction_outcome`) or a refusal with\n * no body at all. So on a Pi box the stamp branch was an inert enforcer and\n * `session health` reported nothing for a session in exactly the state #12374\n * measured (the transport bound abandons the handler, which keeps extracting:\n * `extracted: 0` + `outcome_known: false` on the immediate read, points present\n * seconds later).\n *\n * The DEFER itself is unchanged — this is the Python leg's `_refused` QUESTION\n * (\"did the refusal actually commit, and how did extraction end?\") answered for\n * the one fact this leg needs to make the state visible. The entry keeps\n * retrying.\n *\n * ⚠️ THE STAMP CAN OUTLIVE ITS OWN RESOLUTION, and that is the accepted residual\n * (the Python leg records it too: `tests/test_12379_unrecorded_trace.py`\n * `test_the_unrecorded_marker_is_STICKY_across_a_replay_filing`). Clearing takes a body\n * that RECORDS the ending (`extractionObserved`, or `confirmed` in\n * `{filed, unextracted}`), and the filing a STAMPED entry eventually gets is a\n * REPLAY — its session already exists, so `hosted_api` skips extraction and\n * reports `extracted: 0`, `extraction_mode: \"replayed\"`, which `outcomeRecorded`\n * correctly refuses. A session that resolves after the stamp therefore keeps\n * reading as \"no recorded extraction outcome at the time\" until the entry is\n * evicted, unless a later capture RETRIES a failed one and re-extracts. The\n * health line's \"at the time\" wording is what keeps that honest; it is not a\n * state a client-side predicate can close.\n *\n * FAIL-SAFE: an unreadable body, a non-2xx, a transport error, a turn mismatch,\n * an absent `extraction` block, or any non-boolean `outcome_known` is\n * `\"inconclusive\"` — it neither stamps nor clears. Over-reporting an unknown\n * state costs a false alarm; stamping on an inconclusive read would assert a\n * server state nobody observed, and clearing on one would delete the only trace\n * of a condition that may still hold.\n *\n * ⚠️ ONE DELIBERATE NARROWING vs `_extraction_verdict`, stated so it is not\n * mistaken for an oversight: a detail with NO `extraction` block reads\n * `None` there, which is not `False`, so Python's `UNEXTRACTED` arm fires and it\n * CLEARS. That is #12379's recorded residual for the FILING legs (a pre-#3520\n * server and a malformed body are indistinguishable from a keyless session on\n * that side). This leg does not extend it to a second write path: a body that\n * says nothing about extraction is evidence about nothing, and the marker this\n * read would delete was placed by an OBSERVED `false`. Consequence, one-sided\n * and safe: on a Pi-filing box such an entry keeps the marker where a Python\n * filing of the same body would drop it — a false alarm, never a lost trace.\n *\n * ⛔ It also mirrors `_extraction_verdict`'s PRECEDENCE, not only its PENDING\n * arm: `extracted >= 1` is `FILED` **before** the qualifier is consulted.\n * Points already stored prove extraction RAN — `outcome_known` is\n * `capture_ok IS NOT NULL` and the points are written before that SET\n * (`sdk.py`), so `extracted > 0` with `outcome_known: false` is a **mid- or\n * failed-commit of a capture that DID extract**, not an unknown outcome. Without\n * this clause the two legs would render OPPOSITE verdicts over one shared meta:\n * Python confirms `filed` and CLEARS, this leg would STAMP.\n */\nasync function refusalOutcomeVerdict(\n  cfg: CaptureConfig,\n  sessionId: string,\n  turns: Turn[],\n  fetchImpl: FetchLike,\n  status?: number,\n): Promise<RefusalOutcomeVerdict> {\n  // The Python gate this mirrors is `capture_receipts.may_mint_local_receipt`:\n  // the refusal must be of the RETRYABLE **class** (so a `policy` 401/407 is\n  // excluded — a rotated key or a suspended org is not a post-commit timeout)\n  // AND outside the recorded refusal set `NO_CONFIRM_READ_STATUSES`\n  // (402/403/503). Both halves, or the two legs disagree about which refusals\n  // may be confirmed.\n  if (status !== undefined && (\n    classifyFailure(status) !== \"retry\" ||\n    NO_CONFIRM_READ_STATUSES.includes(status)\n  )) {\n    return \"inconclusive\";\n  }\n  // The WIRE id, not the raw disk value: the payload carried\n  // `normaliseSessionId(...)`, so that is the id the server keyed the session\n  // and its deterministic turn ids on.\n  const wireId = normaliseSessionId(sessionId);\n  if (!wireId || turns.length === 0) return \"inconclusive\";\n  const detail = await getSessionDetail(cfg, wireId, fetchImpl);\n  if (!servedTurnsAreOurCapture(detail, wireId, turns)) return \"inconclusive\";\n  // `_extraction_verdict`'s FIRST arm — see the precedence note above. The clause\n  // is `extractionObserved`, the SAME definition the CLEAR uses, so the two\n  // directions cannot drift; its one documented divergence from\n  // `session_extracted` (a float-spelled count) is stated where it lives.\n  if (extractionObserved((detail as Record<string, unknown>).extracted)) {\n    return \"recorded\";\n  }\n  const block = (detail as Record<string, unknown>).extraction;\n  if (!block || typeof block !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(block)) {\n    return \"inconclusive\";\n  }\n  // ONLY an explicit boolean decides either way. `_extraction_verdict` makes the\n  // same choice for the same reason: an invented `false` would turn garbage into\n  // the PENDING state that the whole trace rests on.\n  const known = (block as Record<string, unknown>).outcome_known;\n  if (known === false) return \"unrecorded\";\n  if (known === true) return \"recorded\";\n  return \"inconclusive\";\n}\n\n/**\n * Replay opportunity. For every spooled session whose content has not been\n * filed, attempt one POST. Transient failures back off; a permanent 4xx is\n * recorded and the entry is marked terminal and KEPT (never unlinked — #7708);\n * entries in a backoff window are skipped (not lost).\n *\n * Filtering keeps the \"deferred extraction\" honest: `onlySessionId` files just\n * the current session at shutdown; `excludeSessionId` drains OTHER sessions\n * while the current one is still live (so a live session is never filed\n * mid-conversation and then silently frozen by the server's session_id replay).\n */\n/**\n * #14350 (D1): ONE drainer per spool — the wrapper that enforces it.\n *\n * An UNTARGETED pass (`onlySessionId` absent) takes the spool's claim and does\n * NOTHING (`heldByClaim`) when another process holds it. Every `session_start`\n * fires a drain, `agent_end` another, shutdown a final flush, and the Python CLI\n * drains the same directory — so N passes used to POST into ONE server ceiling of\n * 8 captures. That is the self-feeding shape #14342 §7.3 measured (908 entries,\n * `attempts` 10–17).\n *\n * ⛔ A TARGETED pass (`onlySessionId`) does NOT take the claim, deliberately:\n * it files ONE named session (the shutdown flush of the LIVE conversation), one\n * POST is the most it can hold, and delaying a session's own filing behind a\n * whole-spool drain is a product regression, not contention control.\n *\n * ⛔ `\"\"` counts as UNTARGETED on both sides of that split — the claim here and\n * the `opts.onlySessionId && …` filter inside the pass — so an empty id can never\n * mean \"drain the whole spool, unclaimed\" (review finding: it did).\n *\n * The claim is released in a `finally` — a pass that throws must not leave the\n * spool locked until the lease expires — and it is refreshed between entries\n * (`renewIfDue`); a `false` from the refresh means a peer took it over, and the\n * pass then STOPS rather than becoming the second drainer it exists to prevent.\n */\nexport async function flushSpool(\n  cfg: CaptureConfig,\n  opts: {\n    dir?: string;\n    fetchImpl?: FetchLike;\n    now?: number;\n    excludeSessionId?: string;\n    onlySessionId?: string;\n    timeoutMs?: number;\n    bounds?: SpoolBounds;\n    inFlight?: Set<string>;\n    maxAttempts?: number;\n    budgetMs?: number;\n    budgetClock?: () => number;\n  } = {},\n): Promise<FlushSummary> {\n  if (opts.onlySessionId) {\n    return flushSpoolPass(cfg, opts, undefined);\n  }\n  const dir = opts.dir ?? SPOOL_DIR;\n  // ⛔ `raced` is the third state `undefined` cannot carry (#14423 review, P2).\n  // `acquireDrainClaim` answers `undefined` for TWO different situations: the\n  // environment cannot hold a claim at all (`mkdir`/write failed — run UNCLAIMED\n  // and say so) and \"both takeover attempts lost a race, the path is momentarily\n  // EMPTY\" — which this function used to read, via `existsSync`, as the first one\n  // and therefore run the pass UNCLAIMED beside a peer that is mid-install, i.e.\n  // the two-drainer defect R1 states cannot happen. The Python twin fails closed\n  // here: ANY `None` from `acquire` becomes `held_by_claim` (`capture_spool.py`).\n  const race: { raced: boolean; obstruction?: string } = { raced: false };\n  const claim = acquireDrainClaim(dir, { leg: \"pi\", state: race });\n  const heldClaim = claim !== undefined;\n  if (!claim) {\n    const record = readDrainClaim(dir);\n    if (record) {\n      const summary = emptyFlushSummary();\n      summary.heldByClaim = true;\n      const who = typeof record.pid === \"number\" ? `pid ${record.pid}` : \"an unnamed process\";\n      const leg = typeof record.leg === \"string\" ? ` (${record.leg} leg)` : \"\";\n      summary.claimHolder =\n        `another drainer holds this spool${leg}: ${who} — nothing attempted`;\n      return summary;\n    }\n    // ⛔ AN UNCLEARABLE OBSTRUCTION IS NOT A HOLDER (checked BEFORE the\n    // can't-read arm below, because that arm's answer — fail closed — is wrong\n    // here): there is no claim record to honour, only something that cannot be\n    // one. Running unclaimed cannot create a second drainer, while reporting\n    // contention names a process that does not exist and wedges the spool FOREVER\n    // — the permanent block #14350 forbids. The reason NAMES the object, in the\n    // same words the Python leg uses, because both legs write the same\n    // `status.json` for the same spool (R6).\n    if (race.obstruction) {\n      const summary = await flushSpoolPass(cfg, opts, undefined);\n      summary.claimUnavailable = true;\n      summary.claimUnavailableReason =\n        `${race.obstruction} exists and is not a claim record (not a regular file) ` +\n        \"and could not be cleared — this pass ran WITHOUT the one-drainer exclusion\";\n      return summary;\n    }\n    // ⛔ SOMETHING AT THE CLAIM PATH THAT WE COULD NOT READ IS NOT \"NOBODY\":\n    // a regular file that a transient error made unreadable (EMFILE, EIO, EACCES)\n    // belongs to a holder that may well be draining right now, and running the\n    // pass UNCLAIMED beside it is the two-drainer defect this mechanism removes.\n    // Fail CLOSED: report it as held, and let the next pass try the read again.\n    if (existsSync(drainClaimPathFor(dir))) {\n      const summary = emptyFlushSummary();\n      summary.heldByClaim = true;\n      summary.claimHolder =\n        \"a claim file exists at this spool but could not be read \" +\n        \"(pid unknown) — nothing attempted\";\n      return summary;\n    }\n    // A lost takeover race with an EMPTY path is NOT \"the claim cannot be created\":\n    // the peer that won is installing. Fail CLOSED exactly as the unreadable case\n    // above does, and let the next pass try again.\n    if (race.raced) {\n      const summary = emptyFlushSummary();\n      summary.heldByClaim = true;\n      summary.claimHolder =\n        \"another drainer won the claim while this pass was acquiring it \" +\n        \"(holder unknown) — nothing attempted\";\n      return summary;\n    }\n    // No claim AND nothing at the path: the claim file could not be CREATED (an\n    // unwritable or unlistable spool root). Run the pass UNCLAIMED and say so — a\n    // root that cannot hold a 60-byte claim file cannot hold a spool entry either,\n    // so the pass can only report the broken spool, and refusing to run would hide\n    // that report (the false-PASS direction is the worse error).\n    const summary = await flushSpoolPass(cfg, opts, undefined);\n    summary.claimUnavailable = true;\n    return summary;\n  }\n  try {\n    const summary = await flushSpoolPass(cfg, opts, claim);\n    // The claim was HELD for this pass. Recorded on the summary so whoever writes\n    // the durable fact knows it may CLEAR a stale `claim_unavailable` — a pass over\n    // an empty spool attempts nothing and would otherwise clear nothing.\n    summary.claimHeld = heldClaim;\n    return summary;\n  } finally {\n    try {\n      claim.release();\n    } catch {\n      /* the lease expires on its own */\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction emptyFlushSummary(): FlushSummary {\n  return {\n    attempted: 0,\n    filed: 0,\n    deferred: 0,\n    skipped: 0,\n    heldByBackoff: 0,\n    heldBack: 0,\n    terminal: 0,\n    remaining: 0,\n    discarded: [],\n  };\n}\n\nasync function flushSpoolPass(\n  cfg: CaptureConfig,\n  opts: {\n    dir?: string;\n    fetchImpl?: FetchLike;\n    now?: number;\n    excludeSessionId?: string;\n    onlySessionId?: string;\n    timeoutMs?: number;\n    bounds?: SpoolBounds;\n    /** Session ids another flush in this process is already handling (#3963). */\n    inFlight?: Set<string>;\n    /** #2100: POST attempts this pass may make (default `FLUSH_MAX_ATTEMPTS`). */\n    maxAttempts?: number;\n    /** #2100: wall-clock budget for this pass (default `FLUSH_BUDGET_MS`).\n     *  Checked BETWEEN entries, never mid-POST, so one slow POST still finishes\n     *  and its outcome is recorded rather than lost. */\n    budgetMs?: number;\n    /** #2100: clock seam for the budget, separate from `now` (which is the\n     *  BACKOFF clock a test may freeze at `1`; sharing it would make every pass\n     *  look instantaneous or infinitely long). */\n    budgetClock?: () => number;\n  } = {},\n  /** #14350: the claim this pass holds, when it holds one (`flushSpool` owns\n   *  acquire/release; `undefined` is a TARGETED pass, which takes none). */\n  claim?: DrainClaim,\n): Promise<FlushSummary> {\n  const dir = opts.dir ?? SPOOL_DIR;\n  const doFetch = opts.fetchImpl ?? (fetch as unknown as FetchLike);\n  const nowMs = opts.now ?? Date.now();\n  const busy = opts.inFlight;\n  const summary: FlushSummary = emptyFlushSummary();\n\n  const maxAttempts =\n    typeof opts.maxAttempts === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(opts.maxAttempts)\n      ? Math.max(0, Math.floor(opts.maxAttempts))\n      : FLUSH_MAX_ATTEMPTS;\n  const budgetMs =\n    typeof opts.budgetMs === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(opts.budgetMs)\n      ? Math.max(0, opts.budgetMs)\n      : FLUSH_BUDGET_MS;\n  const budgetClock = opts.budgetClock ?? (() => Date.now());\n  const startedAt = budgetClock();\n\n  const { metas, discards } = listSpoolEntries(dir);\n  summary.discarded.push(...discards);\n  const ranked = metas.sort(compareAge);\n\n  for (const meta of ranked) {\n    if (opts.onlySessionId && meta.session_id !== opts.onlySessionId) continue;\n    if (opts.excludeSessionId && meta.session_id === opts.excludeSessionId) {\n      summary.heldBack += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (terminalMarker(meta) !== undefined) {\n      // A previous PERMANENT refusal (#7708). The entry is KEPT — terminal,\n      // never destructive — and never retried. Counted separately so a spool\n      // whose entries are all terminal still reports on every flush instead of\n      // reaching the silent steady state `heldByBackoff` was added to close.\n      // The read is TOTAL: a junk non-object marker is read as \"not terminal\"\n      // so a corrupt value can never strand the entry (#8531 review).\n      summary.terminal += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    // Read tolerance: a meta written by an OLDER Python producer stored\n    // `\"filed_lane\": null` for a lane-less filing. The Python leg now OMITS the\n    // key, so both legs write alike — but a spool written before that fix, or\n    // by any producer that emits JSON `null`, must still be read alike, because\n    // JSON has no `undefined`: a raw `JSON.parse` yields `null`, and strict\n    // `===` against this leg's ABSENT key would bypass the skip and re-POST an\n    // entry the other leg had already filed (#3516 §B).\n    // #7708: BOTH sides through ONE normaliser, so a stored falsy lane\n    // (`\"\"`/`0`/`false`) agrees with the `undefined` the payload omits — a raw\n    // `undefined === \"\"` was false and the entry re-POSTed forever (#4714).\n    // #2100: ONE definition of \"filed\", shared with the health count and the\n    // CAS re-read below (see `entryFiled`).\n    if (entryFiled(meta)) {\n      summary.skipped += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    let window = clampWindow(meta.next_attempt_at_ms);\n    if (window > nowMs + RETRY_MAX_MS) {\n      // No legitimately-written window is further out than now + RETRY_MAX:\n      // `backoffDelay` saturates there. Beyond it the value is corrupt, and the\n      // safe reading of an unusable window is \"retry now\" — honouring it would\n      // strand the entry indefinitely.\n      window = 0;\n    }\n    if (window > nowMs) {\n      summary.heldByBackoff += 1;\n      summary.skipped += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (busy?.has(meta.session_id)) {\n      summary.skipped += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    // #2100: the three pass bounds. An entry we refuse to REACH is not\n    // \"skipped\" — it was attemptable and is still queued, so it is counted in\n    // `remaining` and left untouched (its window is neither armed nor reset).\n    // A quota refusal is checked FIRST: once the organization's quota is\n    // exhausted, every further POST in this pass is guaranteed to fail, and\n    // attempting them only deepens each entry's backoff.\n    if (summary.quota) {\n      summary.stopped ??= \"quota\";\n      summary.remaining += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    // #14350 (D1): a CAPACITY refusal is the box's, not this entry's, so the\n    // rest of the pass is guaranteed to meet the same condition — stop before\n    // attempting (and refusing) the remaining entries one by one. Like the quota\n    // arm: `remaining`, never `skipped`, and nothing armed on the entries.\n    if (summary.capacity) {\n      summary.stopped ??= \"capacity\";\n      summary.remaining += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    // #14350: refresh the lease between entries. `false` means a peer took the\n    // claim over after a refresh was missed — stop, rather than become the\n    // second drainer this mechanism exists to prevent.\n    if (claim && !claim.renewIfDue()) {\n      // ⛔ `renew` refuses an EXPIRED record, and the scan above is unbounded and runs\n      // inside the lease — so a scan longer than the TTL expired our OWN claim and\n      // this pass would attempt nothing, forever. `reclaim` re-arms it with the same\n      // compare-and-swap and only while the record is OURS; a real takeover still\n      // stops us (#14423 merge-gate review, mirrored from the Python leg).\n      if (!claim.reclaim()) {\n        summary.lostClaim = true;\n        summary.stopped ??= \"claim\";\n        summary.remaining += 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n    }\n    if (summary.attempted >= maxAttempts) {\n      summary.stopped ??= \"attempts\";\n      summary.remaining += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (budgetClock() - startedAt >= budgetMs) {\n      summary.stopped ??= \"budget\";\n      summary.remaining += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    busy?.add(meta.session_id);\n    try {\n      const turns = readSpoolTurns(dir, meta.session_id);\n      if (turns.length === 0) {\n        // TWO outcomes, decided by whether the log still holds bytes (#7708).\n        // An absent/zero-byte log is provably empty; a log with bytes may be\n        // the only copy of the conversation, so it is RECORDED and KEPT\n        // (terminal) rather than unlinked.\n        summary.discarded.push(\n          turnLogHasContent(dir, meta.session_id)\n            ? quarantineEntry(\n                dir, meta, DISCARD_TRANSCRIPT_TORN,\n                \"turn log has bytes but no parseable turn — kept\", nowMs,\n              )\n            : discardEntry(dir, meta, DISCARD_TRANSCRIPT_EMPTY, \"turn log is empty\"),\n        );\n        continue;\n      }\n\n      summary.attempted += 1;\n      // The digest of what will actually be POSTed (see the CAS below).\n      const postedDigest = contentDigest(turns);\n      const payload = buildCapturePayload({\n        sessionId: meta.session_id,\n        turns,\n        source: meta.source,\n        machineId: meta.machine_id,\n        model: meta.model,\n        // The ENTRY's lane, not this process's: a lane-less import entry\n        // drained here must stay lane-less.\n        captureLane: meta.capture_lane,\n        // #3516 §B / piece 12: the ENTRY's stamp, exactly as it stands — a\n        // lane-less pre-#3516 entry has none and must post without the keys.\n        clientCapturedAt: meta.client_captured_at,\n        clientCapturedAtSource: meta.client_captured_at_source,\n      });\n      const res = await postCapture(cfg, payload, doFetch, opts.timeoutMs);\n      if (res.ok) {\n        // COMPARE-AND-SWAP, on the POSTED CONTENT **AND LANE**. A `turn_end` can\n        // append to this very entry while the POST is in flight (a resumed\n        // session; a cross-process drain). Stamp `filed_key` only when what is\n        // on disk NOW is exactly what was posted; otherwise leave the entry\n        // unfiled so the next opportunity re-posts. Comparing meta-to-meta was\n        // wrong: a writer killed between its log append and its meta write\n        // leaves the meta digest stale, so the check passed while a turn went\n        // unfiled forever.\n        //\n        // The read happens IMMEDIATELY before the write, and the WRITE APPLIES\n        // TO THE FRESHLY-READ META — never to a snapshot taken earlier: writing\n        // an earlier snapshot back would clobber any concurrent edit that landed\n        // in between, including a lane upgrade, which is precisely the edit that\n        // must survive.\n        //\n        // The LANE is part of this identity (#3516 §B): `capture_key` is\n        // content-derived and the lane is not part of the content, so without\n        // that clause a lane upgrade landing inside the POST window was\n        // cancelled and the lane stranded on the wire.\n        //\n        // A RESIDUAL window remains: a lane-upgrading snapshot landing between\n        // the read above and the write below is still clobbered, and the entry\n        // then reads as FILED LANE-LESS until some later snapshot re-triggers\n        // the upgrade (permanent only if the session never snapshots again). It\n        // is accepted rather than closed — the window is two file reads plus one\n        // digest (sub-ms for a typical session, up to ~150 ms at the 16 MB\n        // `SPOOL_MAX_ENTRY_BYTES` bound), and the racer must be the FIRST lane-ful\n        // snapshot of a previously lane-less entry. Closing it needs mutual\n        // exclusion in the capture hot path (every turn_end) in two languages,\n        // where a stale lock would BLOCK OR LOSE CAPTURES — a worse failure. If\n        // it ever must be closed, the lock-free route is a SIDECAR marker file\n        // for the drain-owned fields, so no read-modify-write touches meta.json.\n        const onDisk = readSpoolEntry(dir, meta.session_id);\n        const onDiskTurns = readSpoolTurns(dir, meta.session_id);\n        const postedLane = payload.capture_lane as string | undefined;\n        // #3516 §B: the stamp is part of the posted identity for the same\n        // reason the lane is — metadata, not content — so a stamp appearing\n        // while the POST is in flight must not be cancelled and stranded.\n        const postedStamp = payload.client_captured_at as number | undefined;\n        if (\n          onDisk &&\n          contentDigest(onDiskTurns) === postedDigest &&\n          // #7708: normalised on BOTH sides, like the skip clause — the wire\n          // carried the NORMALISED lane (`postedLane`), so a raw stored\n          // falsy/foreign value could never equal it and the CAS would never\n          // stamp `filed_key` (the re-POST loop).\n          normaliseLane(onDisk.capture_lane) === postedLane &&\n          // Normalised on BOTH sides of the CAS, for the same reason as the skip\n          // clause: `postedStamp` is what the WIRE carried, so the raw disk value\n          // can never equal it.\n          finiteInstant(onDisk.client_captured_at) === postedStamp\n        ) {\n          onDisk.filed_key = onDisk.capture_key ?? captureKey(meta.session_id, onDiskTurns);\n          onDisk.filed_lane = postedLane;\n          // `undefined` is OMITTED by JSON.stringify, which is exactly the\n          // \"the wire carried none\" encoding the skip clause expects.\n          onDisk.filed_stamp = postedStamp;\n          onDisk.filed_at = new Date(nowMs).toISOString();\n          onDisk.attempts = 0;\n          onDisk.next_attempt_at_ms = 0;\n          // #12460: the DURABLE half of the trace, in the SAME CAS as\n          // `filed_key` so it describes the content that was actually filed —\n          // asking the SAME two questions `capture_spool._flush_one` asks\n          // (stamp when the outcome is unrecorded, clear when it is recorded),\n          // answered from the body THIS leg receives: the raw capture receipt,\n          // whose `extracted` count is the evidence — the\n          // synthesised `confirmed` body the Python drain sees never reaches\n          // this leg.\n          //\n          // Before this, the filing path read no response body at all, so this\n          // leg could neither STAMP the marker nor CLEAR it: a session the\n          // server had resolved kept being named by `session health` as \"filed\n          // with no recorded outcome at the time\" until its entry was evicted,\n          // while a Python-side filing of the SAME verdict cleared it. Two legs,\n          // one shared directory, opposite answers about one session.\n          //\n          // ⛔ An UNINFORMATIVE body never clears it (round 1's P1 in #12416's\n          // review): `replayed`, a bare 2xx, a malformed one — each says nothing\n          // about extraction, and a NULL-outcome session replays rather than\n          // re-extracts, so clearing there deletes the only trace while the\n          // condition persists. Only `outcomeRecorded` may clear, and only\n          // `outcomeUnrecorded` may stamp.\n          if (outcomeUnrecorded(res.body)) {\n            onDisk[UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY] = UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_VALUE;\n          } else if (outcomeRecorded(res.body)) {\n            delete onDisk[UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY];\n          }\n          writeMeta(dir, onDisk);\n        }\n        // else: the posted content WAS filed; the entry keeps the newer turns\n        // and stays unfiled, so they are re-posted (and stored) next time.\n        summary.filed += 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n      if (!mayKeepEntry(res.status, res.detail)) {\n        // TERMINAL, NEVER DESTRUCTIVE (#7708): record the refusal and mark the\n        // entry terminal while KEEPING the turn log — `discardEntry` here used\n        // to unlink the only copy.\n        //\n        // The question asked here is the CLASS's (#5051), not\n        // `classifyFailure`'s: a `policy` refusal (401/403/407 — a suspended\n        // org, a rotated key) is NOT terminal, so the entry is kept and\n        // retried once the credential or the org state is fixed (#4895). Only\n        // a genuinely unrecoverable refusal (422, 400, …) terminalises.\n        summary.discarded.push(\n          quarantineEntry(\n            dir,\n            meta,\n            DISCARD_HTTP_REJECTED,\n            `HTTP ${res.status ?? \"none\"}: ${res.detail ?? \"permanent client error\"}`,\n            nowMs,\n          ),\n        );\n        continue;\n      }\n      // #14350 (D1): THE BOX, NOT THE ENTRY. Checked BEFORE the marker read and\n      // before any write, and it does all three things the Python leg does in the\n      // same place: name the cause, charge nothing (`attempts` / `next_attempt_at_ms`\n      // untouched), and end the pass (the `summary.capacity` guard at the top of\n      // the loop stops every later entry). No confirm read either: this entry was\n      // not processed at all, so there is no marker direction to decide.\n      //\n      // ⚠️ Stated asymmetry with the Python drain: that leg may YIELD (honouring\n      // `retry_after`) before giving up, because it runs detached. This leg's pass\n      // is AWAITED on `session_shutdown`, so it must not sleep — it ends, and the\n      // next opportunity continues. Both charge nothing, which is the property\n      // that matters.\n      const capacity = capacityRefusal(res);\n      if (capacity) {\n        summary.capacity = capacity;\n        summary.stopped ??= \"capacity\";\n        summary.deferred += 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n      // #12603: THE MARKER DIRECTION, on the refusal path. A retryable refusal is\n      // not proof that nothing committed (#4675) — the transport bound ABANDONS\n      // its handler rather than cancelling it — so this asks the ONE question the\n      // Python drain's `_refused` asks: are OUR turns stored, and what did the\n      // server record about extraction? Both answers are acted on below — stamp\n      // on `\"unrecorded\"`, clear on `\"recorded\"` — because the Python leg acts on\n      // the same read.\n      //\n      // ⛔ THE READ HAPPENS **BEFORE** THE RE-READ, and that ordering is\n      // load-bearing (both reviewers of this change measured the other order as a\n      // P1). This is a network `await` of up to `CONFIRM_READ_TIMEOUT_MS`; placing\n      // it between the `entryFiled(pending)` guard and `writeMeta` would write\n      // back a snapshot taken BEFORE the await, so a cross-process drain that\n      // filed the entry — or a `turn_end` that appended to it — during the read\n      // would have that write CLOBBERED. The next pass then re-POSTs a session\n      // the server replays as `extracted: 0`, which `outcomeRecorded` correctly\n      // refuses to clear, so a false marker would stand permanently. Fetching\n      // first keeps the read→guard→write pair synchronous, as the pre-#12603\n      // code was.\n      //\n      // ⛔ Only on the RETRYABLE branch (the `mayKeepEntry` guard above): a\n      // permanent refusal and a 2xx filing never pay for this read. ⛔ Bounded by\n      // `CONFIRM_READ_TIMEOUT_MS`.\n      const verdict = await refusalOutcomeVerdict(\n        cfg, meta.session_id, turns, doFetch, res.status);\n      // Re-read before the backoff write-back for the same reason as the CAS\n      // above: never clobber newer turns written while the POST was in flight.\n      const pending = readSpoolEntry(dir, meta.session_id) ?? meta;\n      // #7708: both sides through the normaliser (the same class as the skip\n      // clause and the CAS).\n      if (entryFiled(pending)) {\n        // A CONCURRENT flush already filed this exact content while our POST was\n        // in flight. Re-arming the backoff here would attach a window to content\n        // that was never refused — and since writeSpoolEntry now CARRIES the\n        // window, the next turn's NEW content would inherit it and wait up to\n        // RETRY_MAX_MS with no attempt behind it (#4714 cycle-7 review).\n        summary.skipped += 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n      pending.attempts = clampAttempts(meta.attempts) + 1;\n      pending.next_attempt_at_ms = nowMs + backoffDelay(pending.attempts);\n      // The marker rides the SAME write as the backoff, so a stamped entry can\n      // never be left with an unarmed window. The reverse — an armed window with\n      // no marker — is the ordinary refusal and stays reachable by design.\n      //\n      // Both directions, on the SAME verdict: `\"recorded\"` CLEARS, because the\n      // Python leg's `_refused` turns that same detail into `confirmed: filed`\n      // and `capture_spool._flush_one` clears on it. Leaving the marker standing\n      // here would make one shared meta hold two verdicts at once, and nothing\n      // later would clear it (a stamped entry's eventual filing is a REPLAY,\n      // which records nothing).\n      if (verdict === \"unrecorded\") {\n        pending[UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY] = UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_VALUE;\n      } else if (verdict === \"recorded\") {\n        delete pending[UNRECORDED_OUTCOME_KEY];\n      }\n      writeMeta(dir, pending);\n      summary.deferred += 1;\n      // #2100: name the quota refusal (reporting only — the deferral above is\n      // unchanged, so the capture is still retried on its backoff clock).\n      const quota = quotaRefusal(res);\n      if (quota) summary.quota = quota;\n    } finally {\n      busy?.delete(meta.session_id);\n    }\n  }\n  return summary;\n}\n\n/** Read every turn the spool holds for a session (for tests + diagnostics). */\nexport const PRIOR_META_OK = \"ok\";\nexport const PRIOR_META_ABSENT = \"absent\";\nexport const PRIOR_META_UNUSABLE = \"unusable\";\n\nexport function readSpoolEntry(dir: string, sessionId: string): SpoolMeta | undefined {\n  return readSpoolEntryState(dir, sessionId).meta;\n}\n\n/** The three states of an entry's meta from a WRITER's seat (#12487).\n *  `ok` — read and usable. `absent` — nothing on disk for this session.\n *  `unusable` — SOMETHING IS ON DISK and it could not be read (EACCES/EIO/\n *  EISDIR) or could not be used as a meta (invalid JSON, no usable\n *  `session_id`).\n *\n *  `readSpoolEntry` answers `undefined` for BOTH non-ok states, which is right\n *  for a READER and wrong for the WRITER: `writeSpoolEntry` treated them alike\n *  by building the new entry over `stored = []`, so the log was REWRITTEN from\n *  the snapshot (destroying stored turns) and `filed_key` / `attempts` /\n *  `next_attempt_at_ms` were wiped. Measured on the Python twin, which is the\n *  same code: the log fell from 6 lines to 1 and an already-filed session read\n *  as undelivered. Mirrors `capture_spool._read_prior_meta`.\n */\nexport function readSpoolEntryState(\n  dir: string,\n  sessionId: string,\n): { meta?: SpoolMeta; state: string } {\n  let raw: string;\n  try {\n    raw = readFileSync(metaPathFor(dir, sessionId), \"utf8\");\n  } catch (err) {\n    const code = (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException | undefined)?.code;\n    return { state: code === \"ENOENT\" ? PRIOR_META_ABSENT : PRIOR_META_UNUSABLE };\n  }\n  let meta: unknown;\n  try {\n    meta = JSON.parse(raw);\n  } catch {\n    return { state: PRIOR_META_UNUSABLE };\n  }\n  if (\n    !meta ||\n    typeof meta !== \"object\" ||\n    typeof (meta as SpoolMeta).session_id !== \"string\" ||\n    !(meta as SpoolMeta).session_id\n  ) {\n    return { state: PRIOR_META_UNUSABLE };\n  }\n  return { meta: meta as SpoolMeta, state: PRIOR_META_OK };\n}\n\n/**\n * #2100: does this failed POST mean no further POST in this pass can succeed?\n *\n * `classifyFailure` deliberately keeps 402 TRANSIENT (the identical capture\n * succeeds once a node frees) — that verdict is unchanged. This is the other\n * question, asked for REPORTING: a 402 (or a body carrying one of the server's\n * 402 categories — `quota_exceeded` for the plan allowance, `cohort_cost_cap`\n * for the org's spend cap) means the refusal is the ORGANIZATION's, not this\n * payload's, so attempting the remaining entries only burns the budget and\n * deepens every entry's backoff. Naming it is what turns a silent backlog into\n * `capture: BLOCKED (n queued, 402 quota)`.\n */\nexport function quotaRefusal(res: {\n  status?: number | null;\n  detail?: string;\n}): QuotaRefusal | undefined {\n  const detail = typeof res.detail === \"string\" ? res.detail : \"\";\n  const code = REFUSAL_CODES.find((c) => detail.includes(c));\n  const status =\n    typeof res.status === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(res.status) ? res.status : undefined;\n  if (status !== 402 && code === undefined) return undefined;\n  return {\n    status: status ?? 402,\n    ...(code ? { code } : {}),\n    ...(detail ? { detail } : {}),\n  };\n}\n\n/** The server's machine-readable `detail.code`, or `undefined` (total). */\nfunction serverRefusalCode(detail: string): string | undefined {\n  if (!detail) return undefined;\n  // The transport detail is `HTTP <status> — <body>`, so the JSON is a SUFFIX.\n  const start = detail.indexOf(\"{\");\n  if (start < 0) return undefined;\n  let node: unknown;\n  try {\n    node = JSON.parse(detail.slice(start));\n  } catch {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n  for (let depth = 0; depth < 2; depth += 1) {\n    if (!node || typeof node !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(node)) return undefined;\n    const rec = node as Record<string, unknown>;\n    const code = rec.code;\n    if (typeof code === \"string\" && code !== \"\") return code;\n    node = rec.detail;\n  }\n  return undefined;\n}\n\nfunction statusPathFor(dir: string): string {\n  return join(dir, SPOOL_STATUS_FILE);\n}\n\n/**\n * #14350 (D1): the CAPACITY refusal this failed POST represents, if any.\n *\n * The sibling of `quotaRefusal`, and typed for the same reason: the server\n * publishes `detail.code = \"capture_capacity_saturated\"` precisely so a capture\n * client — which ships independently of the server's prose — can name the\n * condition without matching a message.\n *\n * ⛔ What `flushSpool` does with this is the whole point of D1, and it is NOT\n * what it does with a quota refusal: a quota refusal is REPORTED and the entry\n * deferred on its backoff clock (unchanged); a CAPACITY refusal is evidence\n * about nothing on the spool, so the entry is not charged, and the pass stops.\n * `attempts` then measures what it is for — the entry's own refusals — which is\n * what makes the epic's `attempts <= 3` falsifiable at all.\n */\nexport function capacityRefusal(res: {\n  status?: number | null;\n  detail?: string;\n}): CapacityRefusal | undefined {\n  const detail = typeof res.detail === \"string\" ? res.detail : \"\";\n  const status =\n    typeof res.status === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(res.status) ? res.status : undefined;\n  const typed = detail.includes(CAPTURE_CAPACITY_CODE);\n  // ⛔ THE STATUS IS THE TRIGGER, THE CODE IS THE NAME (the Python leg's rule,\n  // verbatim). Requiring the typed category would leave the measured defect\n  // alive on every deployment that does not send one — the entry would be\n  // charged for a condition that says nothing about it. A 429 IS a saturation\n  // signal by construction.\n  if (status !== 429 && !typed) return undefined;\n  const retryAfter = retryAfterSeconds(detail);\n  return {\n    status: status ?? 429,\n    // ⛔ THE SERVER'S OWN CATEGORY WINS WHEN IT SENT ONE (mirrors\n    // `capture_spool._parsed_refusal_code`). The capture lane has a SECOND 429 —\n    // `ingest_rate_limited`, the per-ORG window — and relabelling it\n    // `capture_capacity_saturated` wrote a cause the server never sent into the\n    // SHARED `status.json`, overwriting the accurate record the Python leg had\n    // just written and telling the operator \"wait for a slot\" when the answer is\n    // \"wait for the org's ingest window\". The status still makes it BOX-level for\n    // the charging rule; the code only NAMES it.\n    code: serverRefusalCode(detail) ?? CAPTURE_CAPACITY_CODE,\n    ...(retryAfter !== undefined ? { retry_after: retryAfter } : {}),\n    ...(detail ? { detail } : {}),\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * The server's `retry_after` out of a refusal body, or `undefined`.\n *\n * TOTAL over every shape — an unparseable body, a scalar, a nested `detail`\n * object, a string, a negative or non-finite number each answer `undefined` — so\n * a corrupt body can never throw inside the per-entry loop.\n */\nfunction retryAfterSeconds(detail: string): number | undefined {\n  if (!detail) return undefined;\n  // ⚠️ THIS LEG'S `detail` IS NOT ALWAYS A BARE JSON BODY: `post()` composes\n  // `HTTP <status> — <json>` for a non-2xx (see `post`), while the Python drain\n  // carries the raw body. So the parse is attempted on the whole string and,\n  // failing that, from the first `{` — otherwise every refusal this leg reports\n  // would silently lose the server's own `retry_after` and the two legs would\n  // disagree about one refusal body.\n  let body: unknown;\n  const candidates = [detail];\n  const brace = detail.indexOf(\"{\");\n  if (brace > 0) candidates.push(detail.slice(brace));\n  for (const candidate of candidates) {\n    try {\n      body = JSON.parse(candidate);\n    } catch {\n      continue;\n    }\n    const found = retryAfterFrom(body);\n    if (found !== undefined) return found;\n  }\n  return undefined;\n}\n\nfunction retryAfterFrom(body: unknown): number | undefined {\n  const candidates: unknown[] = [body];\n  if (body && typeof body === \"object\" && !Array.isArray(body)) {\n    const nested = (body as Record<string, unknown>).detail;\n    if (nested && typeof nested === \"object\" && !Array.isArray(nested)) candidates.push(nested);\n  }\n  for (const candidate of candidates) {\n    if (!candidate || typeof candidate !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(candidate)) continue;\n    const raw = (candidate as Record<string, unknown>).retry_after;\n    let value: number | undefined;\n    if (typeof raw === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(raw)) value = raw;\n    else if (typeof raw === \"string\" && raw.trim() !== \"\") {\n      const parsed = Number(raw);\n      if (Number.isFinite(parsed)) value = parsed;\n    }\n    if (value !== undefined && value >= 0) return value;\n  }\n  return undefined;\n}\n\n// ── #14350 (D1): the cross-process drain claim ────────────────────────\n\nexport function drainClaimPathFor(dir: string): string {\n  return join(dir, DRAIN_CLAIM_FILE);\n}\n\n/** Read the parsed claim, or `undefined`. The SURFACE (drain line, tests).\n *\n * ⛔ THE TOLERANCE HERE IS A SAFETY PROPERTY, not convenience (review finding).\n * `acquireDrainClaim` refuses to take a claim `claimIsStale` judges LIVE; the\n * caller then asks this function whether the refusal was \"somebody holds it\" or\n * \"the spool cannot hold a claim\". A reader STRICTER than the staleness\n * predicate answers `undefined` for a record that is live — and the caller then\n * runs UNCLAIMED beside that holder, which is the two-drainer defect this whole\n * mechanism removes. So the contract is: **whatever `claimIsStale` can judge is\n * readable here.** Token-ness is the surface's business, not this reader's.\n */\nexport function readDrainClaim(dir: string): DrainClaimRecord | undefined {\n  const claimPath = drainClaimPathFor(dir);\n  let text: string;\n  try {\n    // ⛔ `lstatSync` FIRST: reading a FIFO or a socket BLOCKS until a writer\n    // appears, so a corrupt object at the claim path used to hang the drain,\n    // `renew`, the holder line and every health surface forever (measured on the\n    // Python twin; review finding). Only a REGULAR file is read.\n    if (!lstatSync(claimPath).isFile()) return undefined;\n    text = readFileSync(claimPath, \"utf8\");\n  } catch {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n  let parsed: unknown;\n  try {\n    parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n  } catch {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n  if (!parsed || typeof parsed !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(parsed)) return undefined;\n  const record = parsed as Record<string, unknown>;\n  return typeof record.expires_at === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(record.expires_at)\n    ? (record as unknown as DrainClaimRecord)\n    : undefined;\n}\n\n/**\n * Is this process alive on THIS box? Unprovable reads as ALIVE (#14350).\n *\n * ERROR is \"exists, not ours\". Every other failure leaves the question\n * unanswered, and the answer that cannot create a SECOND drainer is \"alive\":\n * honouring a dead claim costs one skipped pass (the next one takes it), while\n * stealing a live one is the defect the claim exists to remove.\n */\nexport function pidAlive(pid: unknown): boolean {\n  if (typeof pid !== \"number\" || !Number.isInteger(pid) || pid <= 0) return true;\n  try {\n    process.kill(pid, 0);\n  } catch (err) {\n    const code = (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code;\n    if (code === \"ESRCH\") return false;\n    return true;\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n\nfunction readBootId(): string | undefined {\n  // Linux only. Absent (macOS, a container without /proc), the pid rule is\n  // skipped and only the lease governs — the safe degradation: the lease still\n  // bounds a dead holder's claim, and the pid rule can never fire on a pid it\n  // cannot disambiguate across reboots.\n  try {\n    return readFileSync(\"/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id\", \"utf8\").trim() || undefined;\n  } catch {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction thisHost(): string {\n  try {\n    return hostname() || \"unknown\";\n  } catch {\n    return \"unknown\";\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * The three staleness rules, as one TOTAL predicate (#14350).\n *\n * 1. UNATTRIBUTABLE — unreadable, not an object, no usable `expires_at`.\n * 2. EXPIRED — `now >= expires_at`. The only rule that applies to a claim from\n *    another host (a copied spool): this box cannot check that host's pids.\n * 3. DEAD HOLDER — same `host` AND same `boot_id` (a rebooted box can reuse a\n *    pid) and `process.kill(pid, 0)` says it is gone.\n *\n * Pure, so every staleness test pins it directly.\n */\nexport function claimIsStale(\n  record: unknown,\n  opts: {\n    nowMs: number;\n    host?: string;\n    bootId?: string;\n    alive?: (pid: unknown) => boolean;\n  },\n): boolean {\n  if (!record || typeof record !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(record)) return true;\n  const rec = record as Record<string, unknown>;\n  // Rule 1 also covers a record whose VERSION this build does not know: a future\n  // format must never be interpreted by today's semantics — the version field\n  // exists to say so, and reading it nowhere made it decoration (review finding).\n  if (rec.version !== DRAIN_CLAIM_VERSION) return true;\n  const expires = rec.expires_at;\n  if (typeof expires !== \"number\" || !Number.isFinite(expires)) return true;\n  if (opts.nowMs >= expires) return true;\n  if (opts.host === undefined) return false;\n  if (rec.host !== opts.host) return false;\n  if (\n    typeof rec.boot_id === \"string\" && rec.boot_id !== \"\" &&\n    typeof opts.bootId === \"string\" && opts.bootId !== \"\" &&\n    rec.boot_id !== opts.bootId\n  ) {\n    return true;\n  }\n  const pid = rec.pid;\n  if (typeof pid === \"number\" && Number.isInteger(pid) && pid > 0) {\n    const alive = opts.alive ?? pidAlive;\n    if (!alive(pid)) return true;\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n\nexport interface DrainClaim {\n  token: string;\n  /** Refresh the lease. `false` means the claim is no longer ours. */\n  renew(nowMs?: number): boolean;\n  /** Refresh only when a third of the TTL has gone (the per-entry call). */\n  renewIfDue(nowMs?: number): boolean;\n  /** Re-arm a lease that lapsed while WE were still the holder (mirror of the\n   *  Python `DrainClaim.reclaim`). `renew` refuses an EXPIRED record on purpose, and\n   *  the pass's one unbounded step — the whole-spool scan — runs inside the lease, so\n   *  a scan longer than the TTL expired our OWN claim and every later pass attempted\n   *  nothing, forever (#14423 merge-gate review). Ownership, not the clock, decides:\n   *  the record on disk must still carry OUR token, and the re-arm is the same\n   *  compare-and-swap `renew` uses, so a real takeover still wins. */\n  reclaim(nowMs?: number): boolean;\n  /** Give the claim up. `false` means we no longer held it. */\n  release(): boolean;\n}\n\n/**\n * Atomically MOVE the claim aside, so the comparison can be trusted.\n *\n * ⛔ THE READ-COMPARE-UNLINK SEQUENCE THIS REPLACES WAS RACY, and the race\n * restored the exact defect the claim exists to remove (review finding, both\n * legs): two processes that both judged the same claim stale both pass a\n * compare, then one installs its own claim while the other is between its read\n * and its unlink — and the second unlink removes the NEW, LIVE claim. Both then\n * hold it, and the loser only finds out at its next refresh.\n *\n * `rename` is the arbiter: exactly one contender can move the file away, and the\n * CONTENT THAT WAS THERE lands in the quarantine — so the caller compares bytes\n * it provably owns the moment of. Returns `[quarantine, raw]`, or `undefined`\n * when the claim was already gone (a peer moved it first; not a failure).\n *\n * ⚠️ EXPORTED AS THE TEST SEAM, mirroring the Python twin, which drives\n * `drain_claim._takedown` directly: the restore branch this pairs with is a\n * two-writer interleaving that a single-threaded caller can never reach through\n * `acquireDrainClaim`, so exporting is the only way to test the real bytes\n * rather than an asserted-away claim.\n */\nexport function takedownClaim(\n  path: string, token: string,\n): { quarantine: string; raw: string } | undefined {\n  // ⚠️ The `.tmp-` INFIX is load-bearing: `sweepStaleTempFiles` collects\n  // abandoned `*.tmp-*` files by mtime, and a taker killed between the rename and\n  // the disposal would otherwise leave a file in the spool root nothing ever\n  // collects. The quarantine lives for microseconds; the sweep bound is minutes.\n  const quarantine = `${path}.tmp-take-${process.pid}-${token}`;\n  try {\n    renameSync(path, quarantine);\n  } catch {\n    return undefined;\n  }\n  let raw: string;\n  try {\n    raw = readFileSync(quarantine, \"utf8\");\n  } catch {\n    try { unlinkSync(quarantine); } catch { /* best effort */ }\n    return undefined;\n  }\n  return { quarantine, raw };\n}\n\n/**\n * Drop a quarantined claim, or PUT IT BACK if it was not the one we judged.\n *\n * `true` means the bytes really were `expected` and the claim is gone. `false`\n * means we displaced somebody else's NEW claim — a third process installed it\n * between our rename and our write — so it is restored by `linkSync`, which\n * cannot replace a claim a FOURTH process has since installed.\n *\n * ⛔ WHAT HAPPENS WHEN EVEN THE RESTORE LOST — stated accurately, because the\n * earlier version of this sentence (\"the displaced holder … stops at its next\n * refresh — the safe direction\") was FALSE, and a false sentence is worse than no\n * sentence: the displaced holder's claim file is GONE, so it keeps draining until\n * its next `renew` tells it the claim is not ours — up to `ttlMs / 3` away. Two\n * drainers can coexist for that window, and it is BOUNDED BY THE LEASE rather than\n * unbounded, which is the only claim this code makes. The failure is therefore\n * WARNED rather than swallowed: a silently deleted live claim is exactly the class\n * this module exists to make visible. Mirrors `drain_claim._dispose_quarantine`.\n *\n * ⚠️ EXPORTED AS THE TEST SEAM — see `takedownClaim`.\n */\nexport function disposeQuarantine(\n  quarantine: string, raw: string, path: string, expected: string,\n  //: TEST seams with the module defaults, for the same reason `restoreQuarantine`\n  //: takes them: the failure arms cannot be built portably otherwise.\n  link: (from: string, to: string) => void = linkSync,\n  move: (from: string, to: string) => void = renameSync,\n): boolean {\n  if (raw !== expected) {\n    try {\n      link(quarantine, path);\n    } catch (err) {\n      const code = (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code;\n      if (code === \"EEXIST\") {\n        warn(\n          `a displaced drain claim could not be restored at ${path} ` +\n            `(${code}); its holder keeps draining until its next refresh (up to ttl/3)`,\n        );\n      } else {\n        // ⛔ THE ONLY OTHER COPY (review, cycle 16, P1): the rename vacated `path`, so on a\n        // non-EEXIST failure this quarantined file is all that is left of a live\n        // holder's claim. Deleting it turns \"restore failed\" into \"claim destroyed\"\n        // and frees the path for the next `linkSync`. It is not merely spared: while\n        // the path is still FREE the object is renamed BACK (`renameSync` needs no\n        // hard link — `tox-dev/filelock#843`), and only when that also fails is the\n        // copy left in place (BOUNDED — the `*.tmp-*` shape is swept by mtime).\n        let pathFree = false;\n        try { lstatSync(path); } catch { pathFree = true; }\n        if (pathFree) {\n          try {\n            move(quarantine, path);\n            return false;\n          } catch (renameErr) {\n            warn(\n              `a displaced drain claim could not be restored at ${path} ` +\n                `(${code ?? String(err)}; rename: ${\n                  (renameErr as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code ?? String(renameErr)\n                }) and is PRESERVED at ${quarantine}; its holder keeps draining ` +\n                \"until its next refresh (up to ttl/3)\",\n            );\n            return false;\n          }\n        }\n        warn(\n          `a displaced drain claim could not be restored at ${path} ` +\n            `(${code ?? String(err)}) — the path is OCCUPIED, so the copy is ` +\n            `PRESERVED at ${quarantine}; its holder keeps draining until its next ` +\n            \"refresh (up to ttl/3)\",\n        );\n        // ⛔ AND THE WARNING MUST BE TRUE: this arm used to warn `PRESERVED` and then\n        // FALL THROUGH to the `unlinkSync` below, destroying the only copy of the\n        // displaced holder's claim — the code contradicting its own sentence, which is\n        // this PR's subject. The Python twin returns inside the same arm\n        // (`_dispose_quarantine`), so the fall-through was also a leg divergence\n        // (review, cycle 16, P1).\n        return false;\n      }\n    }\n    // ⛔ FALLING THROUGH IS REACHABLE TWO WAYS, AND ONLY ONE IS A SUCCESSFUL LINK.\n    // ⚠️ Dated rather than round-cited on purpose: the finding was raised by the\n    // confirmation review at `08fd0891`, and a `(review, cycle N, Pn)` tag that names no\n    // row of this record is exactly the dead citation the record itself polices:\n    //   • `linkSync` SUCCEEDED — the displaced claim is back at the path, so this copy\n    //     is a duplicate and is swept;\n    //   • `linkSync` failed EEXIST — a newer claim already holds the path, so this copy\n    //     of a SUPERSEDED claim is swept. The Python twin's `except FileExistsError`\n    //     arm does the same (\"the displaced record's bytes are gone, and the only\n    //     honest thing left is to say so\"), and its warning never says PRESERVED.\n    // The two PRESERVED arms above both RETURN, which is what keeps THAT warning true.\n    try { unlinkSync(quarantine); } catch { /* best effort */ }\n    return false;\n  }\n  try { unlinkSync(quarantine); } catch { /* best effort */ }\n  return true;\n}\n\n/**\n * Put a quarantined object BACK, never replacing a newer one.\n *\n * `linkSync` is the create-if-absent arbiter, so a claim a peer installs while this\n * object sits in quarantine SURVIVES at the path and only the quarantined copy is\n * dropped; `renameSync` would replace it, the \"both processes win\" hazard\n * `takedownClaim` exists to avoid.\n *\n * This is the disposal for the one object neither leg can judge: a REGULAR file at\n * the claim path whose bytes could not be read. It is a possible holder (the\n * doctrine is \"an unprovable read is ALIVE\"), so it is honoured rather than\n * deleted — see the call site in `clearUnusableClaimPath` (#14480 review, F3).\n *\n * ⛔ THE FAILURE PATH MUST NOT DELETE THE OBJECT EITHER. The first cut unlinked the\n * quarantine on ANY `linkSync` failure but `EEXIST`, and the claim path had already\n * been VACATED by the caller's `rename` — so the peer's record existed nowhere\n * while the peer still believed it held, and this process won the next `linkSync`:\n * the two-holder window this mechanism exists to close, with the only copy of the\n * evidence destroyed and the warning on a stderr a session discards (#14480\n * cycle-17 review, P3).\n *\n * A filesystem that refuses hard links (`EPERM`/`EXDEV`/`EMLINK`/`ENOSYS`) is the\n * reachable case, and it has a restore that needs no link: `rename` the object back\n * **while the claim path is still free** — the fallback `filelock`'s stale break\n * takes for exactly this reason (`tox-dev/filelock#843`). `rename` replaces, so it\n * is used ONLY after `lstat` says the path is empty.\n *\n * Returns `true` when the object is back at `path` or a NEWER object holds it, and\n * `false` when it could not be put back — in which case the quarantined copy is\n * LEFT IN PLACE, never unlinked. Mirrors `drain_claim._restore_quarantine`.\n * ⛔ The `link` and `move` parameters are TEST seams with the module defaults: a\n * filesystem that refuses hard links cannot be built portably in a test, and the\n * arm that matters is what happens when it does. `move` is the same seam the\n * caller already takes for its own `rename`, so a test can stand inside the\n * restore's fallback too.\n */\nexport function restoreQuarantine(\n  quarantine: string,\n  path: string,\n  link: (from: string, to: string) => void = linkSync,\n  move: (from: string, to: string) => void = renameSync,\n): boolean {\n  try {\n    link(quarantine, path);\n  } catch (err) {\n    const code = (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code;\n    // EEXIST is the normal case: a newer object already holds the path, and that is\n    // a holder either way. ANY other errno means the object we could not read could\n    // NOT be put back — and since the rename already vacated `path`, the quarantined\n    // file is then the ONLY copy of a live holder's claim.\n    //\n    // ⛔ SO IT IS NOT DELETED (review, cycle 16, P1). This used to warn and then `unlinkSync`\n    // unconditionally, which destroyed that only copy and answered `held` over an\n    // EMPTY path: the next `linkSync` wins and two holders coexist for up to\n    // `ttl/3`, silently — the window the quarantine rule exists to close, with the\n    // moved-aside evidence gone too. `link(2)` lists\n    // EACCES/EDQUOT/EIO/EMLINK/ENOSPC/EPERM/EROFS/EXDEV beside EEXIST, and\n    // ENOSPC/EDQUOT on a spool directory is the ordinary case, not the exotic one.\n    if (code === \"EEXIST\") {\n      try { unlinkSync(quarantine); } catch { /* best effort */ }\n      return true;\n    }\n    let pathFree = false;\n    try { lstatSync(path); } catch { pathFree = true; }\n    if (pathFree) {\n      // No hard link on this filesystem, and the path is still free: put the\n      // object BACK rather than strand a live holder's record.\n      try {\n        move(quarantine, path);\n        return true;\n      } catch (renameErr) {\n        warn(\n          `an unreadable drain claim could not be restored at ${path} ` +\n            `(${code ?? String(err)}; rename: ${\n              (renameErr as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code ?? String(renameErr)\n            }) and is PRESERVED at ${quarantine}; it is honoured as a holder, so ` +\n            \"this pass must not run beside it\",\n        );\n        return false;\n      }\n    }\n    warn(\n      `an unreadable drain claim could not be restored at ${path} ` +\n        `(${code ?? String(err)}) — the path is OCCUPIED, so it is not replaced — ` +\n        \"and the \" +\n        `copy is PRESERVED at ${quarantine}; it is honoured as a holder, so this ` +\n        \"pass must not run beside it\",\n    );\n    return false;\n  }\n  try { unlinkSync(quarantine); } catch { /* best effort */ }\n  return true;\n}\n\n/**\n * Remove something at the claim path that is not a readable claim.\n *\n * A DIRECTORY, a socket or a dangling symlink there is corruption, not a holder:\n * `linkSync` fails `EEXIST` at it, nothing reads it as a claim, and leaving it\n * blocks every future drain forever — the \"a stale claim is re-verifiable, not a\n * permanent block\" rule, failing in its worst direction.\n *\n * A symlink is removed WITHOUT following it, so this cannot reach outside the\n * spool. A non-empty directory is left alone (`ENOTEMPTY`): deleting unknown\n * content is the worse error, and the caller's answer is to report the spool HELD\n * with no holder named — `flushSpool`'s `existsSync` arm, not `claimUnavailable` —\n * and retry on the next pass, so the corruption is surfaced once per pass rather\n * than silently drained beside. (An earlier version of this comment said \"run\n * unclaimed and say so\", which is the arm for a root that cannot hold a claim at\n * all — the opposite direction; corrected in the #14423 cycle-7 review, and the\n * Python twin `drain_claim._clear_unusable_claim_path` has the same polarity.)\n * Mirrors that function, which discriminates on `S_ISDIR` and `unlink`s EVERYTHING\n * else.\n *\n * ⛔ THE OBJECT IS MOVED ASIDE ATOMICALLY, AND THE OBJECT THAT LANDED IN THE\n * QUARANTINE IS WHAT THIS CALL JUDGES. The `lstat` → re-read `(dev, ino)` → `unlink`\n * version this replaces NARROWED the check-then-unlink window without closing it:\n * between the re-read and the `unlink` a peer can replace the object with its own\n * VALID claim, and the `unlink` — which is by NAME, not by inode — deletes that\n * claim, after which this process wins the next `link` and two holders coexist for\n * up to `ttl/3`, silently (#14423 cycle-9 review, P2). An inode compare is not sound\n * evidence on its own either: ext4/xfs hand a freed inode straight to the next file,\n * so \"same dev+ino\" can be a DIFFERENT live lockfile — the `filelock` advisory\n * GHSA-fq56-3cx3-fjxp admits two holders exactly that way. `rename` has neither\n * problem: exactly one contender can move the file, the CONTENT THAT WAS THERE is\n * what lands in the quarantine, and the judgement is over bytes this call provably\n * owns the moment of. Same arbiter as `takedownClaim` (and the Python twin\n * `drain_claim._clear_unusable_claim_path`), and the consensus outside this repo:\n * `flufl.lock`, git's lockfile API, and the eviction protocol that renames a stale\n * lock to a unique path and reports a race rather than unlinking.\n */\n//: The answers `clearUnusableClaimPath` must be able to give — a BOOLEAN could\n//: not, and collapsing two of them into `false` was a live two-drainer hole\n//: (#14423 cycle-8 review, P1): `GONE` (the object vanished under us — a peer\n//: released or replaced it) must make the caller RETRY, never answer \"cannot be\n//: cleared\", because that sent `flushSpool` down the unclaimed-pass arm while the\n//: peer's claim sat on disk. `REFUSED` is what \"unclearable\" actually means.\n//: `HELD` is the fourth: the object moved aside turned out to be a REGULAR file, so\n//: a peer's claim appeared in the window and was put back — there IS a holder to\n//: honour, which is CONTENTION, not \"unavailable\" (#14480 review, F2: reporting it\n//: as unavailable named an unwritable root that was perfectly writable).\nexport type ClaimPathClear = \"gone\" | \"refused\" | \"done\" | \"held\";\n\n/**\n * Exported for its own unit tests, which drive the `rename` seam below: the\n * judged-object-property and the `ENOENT`-means-`gone` answer are the two\n * properties this function exists for, and neither can be observed without\n * interleaving.\n * ⛔ The `rename` parameter is a TEST seam with the module default — the same\n * interleave the Python leg's tests drive by monkeypatching `os.rename`.\n */\nexport function clearUnusableClaimPath(\n  path: string,\n  rename: (from: string, to: string) => void = renameSync,\n  //: The restore's `link` seam, for the same reason `rename` is a seam: a\n  //: filesystem that refuses hard links cannot be built in a test, and the arm\n  //: that matters is what the restore does when the link is refused.\n  link: (from: string, to: string) => void = linkSync,\n  //: The junk-disposal seam, for the same reason `rename` and `link` are seams: a\n  //: filesystem whose `unlink` refuses (`EIO`/`EACCES`/`EROFS`) cannot be built in\n  //: a test, and the arm that matters is what the clear answers when only the\n  //: ASIDE copy cannot be deleted (#14480 review at `1a0c29c3`, P2).\n  unlink: (target: string) => void = unlinkSync,\n): ClaimPathClear {\n  let before: ReturnType<typeof lstatSync>;\n  try {\n    before = lstatSync(path);\n  } catch (err) {\n    return (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code === \"ENOENT\" ? \"gone\" : \"refused\";\n  }\n  if (before.isFile()) return \"refused\";  // a possible holder: never remove one here\n  if (before.isDirectory()) {\n    try {\n      rmdirSync(path);  // an EMPTY directory; refuses ENOTEMPTY otherwise\n      return \"done\";\n    } catch (err) {\n      return (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code === \"ENOENT\" ? \"gone\" : \"refused\";\n    }\n  }\n  // Everything else — a symlink, a FIFO, a socket, a device — is MOVED ASIDE\n  // first. `rename` is the CAS: after it, whatever this call is about to drop is\n  // whatever it can see, and no peer can swap it out from under the decision.\n  // ⚠️ The `.tmp-` infix is load-bearing: the scratch sweeper collects abandoned\n  // `*.tmp-*` files under the spool root by mtime.\n  const quarantine = `${path}.tmp-clear-${process.pid}-${randomToken()}`;\n  try {\n    rename(path, quarantine);\n  } catch (err) {\n    return (err as NodeJS.ErrnoException)?.code === \"ENOENT\" ? \"gone\" : \"refused\";\n  }\n  let landedRegular = false;\n  let landedRaw: string | undefined;\n  try {\n    landedRegular = lstatSync(quarantine).isFile();\n    landedRaw = landedRegular ? readFileSync(quarantine, \"utf8\") : undefined;\n  } catch {\n    landedRaw = undefined;\n  }\n  if (landedRaw !== undefined) {\n    // ⛔ A PEER'S CLAIM LANDED IN THE WINDOW. It was not a claim when we looked and\n    // it is one now, so it is put BACK (`linkSync` cannot replace a newer object)\n    // and the answer is `held`: there is a holder to honour, which is not the same\n    // thing as an unclearable path.\n    disposeQuarantine(quarantine, landedRaw, path, \"<not a claim we judged>\");\n    return \"held\";\n  }\n  if (landedRegular) {\n    // ⛔ A REGULAR FILE WE COULD NOT READ IS A POSSIBLE HOLDER, NOT JUNK.\n    // `landedRaw` is `undefined` for TWO different facts and only one of them says\n    // \"drop it\": *not a regular file* (a symlink/FIFO/socket/device — corruption)\n    // and *a regular file whose bytes could not be had* (`EMFILE`, `EIO`, `EACCES`\n    // after a mode drift). The second is a PEER'S CLAIM that arrived in the window;\n    // `unlink`ing it lets this process win the next `linkSync`, so two holders\n    // coexist for up to `ttlMs / 3` — silently. That is the class the `filelock`\n    // advisory GHSA-fq56-3cx3-fjxp describes, and it inverts the doctrine\n    // `acquireDrainClaim` states below: a regular claim we could not read is\n    // HONOURED (`undefined` = somebody holds it) and never unlinked\n    // (#14480 review, F3). The Python twin carries the same branch, and the\n    // restore itself never deletes the object it promised to honour: a refused\n    // `linkSync` renames it back while the path is free, and preserves it\n    // otherwise (cycle-17 review, P3).\n    restoreQuarantine(quarantine, path, link, rename);\n    return \"held\";\n  }\n  try {\n    unlink(quarantine);\n  } catch (error) {\n    // ⛔ `refused` here would be a lie, and an expensive one (review at\n    // `1a0c29c3`, P2 — reproduced on the Python twin: `refused` while the claim\n    // path was already